Parliament didn't support an amendment to release Tymoshenko
The Ukrainian Parliament today rejected the proposal to repeal the amendment to the section of the Criminal Code condemning Opposition Leader Yulia Tymoshenko.
The modification would open the door to the release of the former Prime Minister, but parliamentarians rejected the proposal to reduce penalties for economic crimes at the second reading.
In October, Tymoshenko was sentenced to seven years in prison for the alleged signing of a disattlerated agreement with Russia for Ukraine on the purchase of natural gas.
Sentencing has not yet been final, the appellate court is required to review it in December.
Tymoshenko was convinced that it was a political revenge of the regime, and even in the West doubts have arisen about the impartiality of the court.
The proposal to withdraw paragraph 365 of the Criminal Code with which the former Prime Minister was convicted was supported by 147 deputies in the vote.
226 votes were required for their approval.
