Begins a new era of the crisis
France and the Rest of Europe Go Hunt for Invest Investor Search
A whirlwind stunt has spread across Europe
Individual states have published their GDP data for Q3.
The Czech economy has stagnated, while France and Germany have grown. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 
However, the bond market today has broken records.
Investors will no longer only focus on Italy, but also in Spain, France, Austria and others.
The statistical offices of the Eurozone, Germany, the Czech Republic and other European countries have already published their preliminary estimates of economic growth in the third quarter.
Germany &apos; s GDP grew by 0.5 per cent, France's 0.4 per cent
Both countries have made public the data for the second four-month period, in Germany it has improved more than expected (growth of 0.3 per cent rather than the expected 0.1%), although in France there was a slight decrease of 0.1 per cent (original data spoke of stagnation).
The Czech economy grew year-on-year by 1.5% in the third quarter of this year.
However, in the four-quarter period, gross domestic product (GDP) has stagnated and according to the "YES" the trend towards a gradual slowdown in the growth of economic development is formed.
The results are even worse than analysts' forecasts.
Main Events:
The Czech economy grew 1.5 per cent in the third trimester of this year, but quadrupled.
In the third trimester in the third quarter the German economy, according to previous and seasonally recalculated data, grew by 0.5 per cent.
The economy of the Eurozone and also of the European Union, between July and September and compared to the previous quarter, grew by 0.2%, in line with the expectations of analysts.
Greece's economy has ploughed 5.2 per cent year-on-year.
The interest on its 10-year bonds is 28.45%.
The interest of Italian and Spanish bonds has grown to critical limits.
The problematic situation also begins to affect Austria, the Netherlands, France, Belgium, etc.
Ten-year-old Czech bond interest remains at 4 per cent, although in the area the situation is worsening.
In Germany there are loud voices that say the ECB should ultimately grant loans.
