In Need for Speed: The Run is racing all over America.
Review
A journey, in principle carefree, is complicated by a series of improper neglects of a routine trip during tomorrow's shift.
To a certain extent it was possible to meet the expectations, but this is of no use, if, in reality, it was not intended to do anything extraordinary.
The new installment, to which The Run is added to the title, aroused great expectations already before its premiere.
He voried that he would give new air to the old racing saga Need for Speed racing saga and, following what was shown, appears as a game that shouldn't end up being a genre of action again.
We are also curious to know how the scenes would look, where the main character descends from the car and walks on his own feet.
As is usually the case in the NFS series, the results don't vary much about what we were expecting.
There are races that can be considered acceptable, but in the total computation points are lost due to absurd flaws, which have already been appearing in the series for years.
You can't really even call mistakes, but it's rather the incomprehearted disgrace of programmers.
Adventure begins very well.
You are introduced to a story, whose protagonist, Jack, has certain personal problems; to solve them, he is offered to participate in an illegal career throughout America.
Stay behind the wheel on the west coast in San Francisco, and your mission is to get the first ones in New York.
It's the idea to offer something interesting.
The route is divided into ten stages and then into more than fifty racing.
It follows that each race takes place by a different route and the stages are located in different environments, which are quite diverse and interesting.
In this way, you don't even remotely have the feeling that the game always offers the same thing.
Some of the environments are even pleasant in sight, although the technical level of the graph is equivalent to that of previous installments of the series.
It's therefore nothing from the other world.
But stroll through the wilderness, the stunning landscape or the snow-covered mountains produce a very pleasant visual effect.
In addition, racing is diversified from time to time by an atypical element.
So, for example, in the desert you find a sandstorm that decreases the visibility.
On the other hand, in the mountains you have to be careful so that an avalanche doesn't bury your avalanche.
According to that, every racing is very well practiced.
There's only a couple of models, but that's enough.
For a moment, you're trying to overtake a number of required rivals (normal in a classic race), then try to gain time (classic chronometers) and then expect a duel against a single opponent.
In the midst of all of this sometimes the police appear, which only makes things even more difficult.
The novelty is the scenery outside the car.
Interactive animations are presented when Jack, in most cases, has to escape from someone and you have to help him, always pressing the button just at the right time.
It's not complicated.
Unsurprisingly, however, that there are plenty of fewer scenery in the game than you'd expect (there's only just three).
But the final result is positive, because this element doesn't become annoying and, in the context already mentioned, it is one of the elements that make the game more interesting.
The driving model follows in the footsteps of the previous episodes.
That means it's no longer a big deal today, but you can't say it's bad.
If you're searching for a realistic simulation, you can already search somewhere else.
But a race concocted with a lot of action suits this style of driving, although we will be able to find something better between the games of the competition.
So far, it's all in order.
But the problems arise after a certain period of time.
Contrary to the correctness of the driving models, there is no longer the same with the collisions.
Gambling of this kind cannot be expected to recreate tricky deformations and colliding, but in fact before a knock against any object, you can't predict how your car will react, so it's not all in order.
It's typical that, by making a very tight advance or circulating the car with a normal traffic, there are only slight frictions, but in other almost identical situations, your car ends up doing uncontrollable pirouettes.
Either it's even utterly destroyed.
Thanks to the reset system, no major problems arise, but it can put you in a bad mood.
Resettles are programmed in such a way that, when you're having an accident or you get off the track, the game restores you to the last ransom point on the track.
However, in each race there are only five disposals available.
If you consume them all, you have to restart the race from the very beginning.
So it's just help in case something doesn't go well, but it's not very reliable.
At times, neither resettling helps, it only helps to learn the given stage of memory.
A great disadvantage of the game is that the same facts are repeated in different places.
In a few curves, for example, you have to enter in a specific way; otherwise, they are impossible to take.
At other times you'll find "civilian" vehicles somewhere always with the same set-up, even if you're the first, fifth or you arrive five minutes before or after.
This serves to rage above all in situations, when you see that two cars that go to meet in opposite directions, your opponent advances without problems, but when you arrive, you have to turn or stop.
It's the same when you get there, because it's programmed that "surprise" will always happen under your nose.
Opponents also have one more advantage.
During the whole game you will go to places where, without taking into account the moment, the same opponent will always advance to you in the same place.
In addition this happens at the end of the game, on a long bridge, which is even more shocking.
Your opponent must come before you, at whatever price.
When it's in front of you, it's difficult for you to see the cars that are coming from the front, so at the beginning you can make the car snaps.
One can understand the idea of creating a game with maximum doses of suspense and adrenaline, but the execution is so apparent that you will surely disappoint right away.
And it's a pity, because even if the travel model is simple, the game is fun and with action, so it's oftenn't possible to follow in detail what's happening or where.
And it really doesn't matter, if the credit comes to the idea with fifty exclusive tracks or ranking in the racing so it doesn't suck the boring, or anything else.
An unusual idea is changing cars during racing.
You have to be attentive to gasoline dispensers on the side of the road, because it's right there you have to change cars, although in the vast majority of cases what you've got.
The cars are divided into three categories.
Sports are suitable for urban areas, U.S. strong cars for straight motorways and exotic super-sports for technical driving, for example, on curved mountain roads.
In addition the main narrative thread there is also a thread of parallel challenges, i.e. there are races in concrete places that you will know from the stories.
Here you will obtain not only medals, but also points of experience for your profile, which serves as much for challenges and stories.
Upon reaching the higher level, the driver opens several possibilities, starting with the car and finishing funds for the profile.
At the same time, there's an online Autolog feature that compares your results to those of other players and develops other community features.
It's one of the toughest elements to understand is how the last race in history is made.
With the car you get to the Metro Tunnel (pass), where you start running in the dark on the tracks (pass).
The velocimeter marks between 150 and 200 km/h (walk) and, all of a sudden, the subway convoy arrives from behind and you're just ahead.
In the newsroom they will surely have a discussion about this for several days, about whether during the programming of the game none even thought a little or if the authors really take us for a bunch of fools.
The technical part of the game is nothing extraordinary, but at the very least the general visual effects are in most cases pleasant, sometimes even proud.
It's a pity that, in the tensiest moments the image quality is diminished and, at times, in a serious way.
This mainly affects accidents involving many entrants, when continuous image quality is no longer valid.
Audio is okay and the soundtrack is a matter of tastes.
In order for the majority of the youth group to the liking, the soundtrack belongs to the category "each of his father and his mother," but it can be assumed that no one would buy the game just because of their music.
The final sensation of Need for Speed: The Run is quite confusing.
If the game avoided absolutely silly mistakes, we'd have trouble claiming The Run is the best NFS game in recent years.
It gives a feeling that the game's unfinished.
The fun is assured, but prepare yourself because the mistakes will hit you in the eyes with the strength of airport reflectors.
The choirs of the Carolina University will participate in the "Vegetive Students" concert.
The joint celebration of the International Day of Students and the Anniversary of the Velvet Revolution as a musical experience will take place for the third time at the Festa Academica festival.
Twenty corals from high school and university centers will be presented.
The festival revolves around two gigs on November 17.
"Carry University has a high participation in the festival."
Among the current choirs are the choral of the faculty of Pedagogy and the Faculty of Humanities, in the committee of the festival is Rector Václav Hampl," said the head of the organization, Jakub Ealoun.
The summit of the entire festival is the free entrance for all audiences to the high school choir concert, which will be held on March 17 at 2 p.m. in front of the National Technical Library in Dejvice.
The program has the participation of the Czech jazzist Karel Roika and his Celebration Jazz Mass.
In the evening will be celebrated at the celebration venues of the Municipal House in Vynohrady the concert "Stunts of Velvet," in which university choirs will participate, including the choir of Uninversidad Carolina.
It aims not only to celebrate the International Day of Students with dignifiedly and to commemorate the 22nd anniversary of the Velvet Revolution, but also to present choral music to the public at the highest level and to promote the collaborative creation among young people.
The main organizer is the Czech Coral Union in collaboration with the Jan Kepler Secondary Institute.
All the events were supported by the Mayor of Prague, Bohuslav Svoboda, and the Rector of the Carolina University, Václav Hampl.
The Festival will take place between 16 and 20 November between Prague and Pardubice.
The Calendar offers, in addition to the celebratory concerts, a whole series of musical events, improvised student spectacles and morning concerts.
S interested parties will be able to find more information at www.festaacademica.cz
U.S. Ambassador to the U.S., Vicenová:
The Czechs have a renegastivist and Eurosceptical reputation.
"The Lisbon Treaty has significantly affected the balance between the individual institutions."
"I wasn't expecting an unrestrained war to mount".
"It will last five, six years, before the situation stabilizes a little, calms down and the new players get used to their new roles," the Czech Ambassador to the Czech Ambassador to the EU, Milena Vicenová, began with vitivism his conference at the Faculty of Law.
How not to take his motto "clearly and outspoken about Europe seriously," which he already stated in the first part of his conference, when he managed to describe every organ of the EU and its relations with such precision and clarity, because it would be the envy of many lecturers at the Faculty of Law of the U. Carolina.
Vice-Nova also weighed the work of the new permanent President of the European Council, Herman van Rompuy:
"Opcriticism emerges, that if it's foolish, a grey civil servant, capable of assuming any role, but I have to say he left everyone baffled, because he assumes his roles perfectly."
"He is a man who knows how to listen to the members of the twenty-seven states, communicate with them and who has a very clear view of where the EU should go."
The new position of representative of Foreign and Security Policy is explained from the question: "Who is this EU? To what number can I call it?" It is therefore an important step towards unifying and clarifying the political union for states such as China or India.
No one wants to ban us Olomouc Cheese.
Vice-Nova has also taken care of the agenda and will follow the European Council in the near future.
The Czech presidency of two years ago was a success.
The Czechs dealt with the emerging economic crisis, resolved the gas crisis and organized a summit for the Eastern Partnership.
The fall of the government at the time was identified as a very negative aspect, which seriously affected the Czech Republic.
The Czech representation in the EU for the time being has many commitments.
In the words of the ambassador, "a good fuss," not only the presidency, but also the financial crisis, which is now being dealt with.
On the other hand, we have to approve the seven-year budget of the EU, caused by the current much more complicated economic situation.
If the budget is simpler and more effective, what sectors will have a priority, which countries will receive the most support and what else? That is something that will be present on the agenda of the next meeting.
The ambassador also recalled her main slogans, with which she arrived in Europe: "About Europe clearly."
"I don't really like ugly abbreviations, quirkiness or jargon that people don't understand."
"How can a citizen understand that cucucumbers don't really invent cucucumbers or round cucumbers, bananas of various sizes or that no one is going to ban Olomouc's cheese?"
It also aims to take more advantage of the EU's environment and bring more Czech citizens closer to the European institutions.
Europe of velocity awaits us
According to the ambassador, the Czechs are renowned for Eurosceptics and people perceive rather the negative aspects than the positive.
He also lamented the low participation of the Czechs in the European institutions.
Guilt lies with a multitude of factors, such as the modesty of our nominees, their inability to write messages of motivation, but also the lack of support from the Czechs.
In addition to the State Presidency &apos; s success, he highlighted the strength and collaboration of the Visegrad Group.
"Believe me that when a Pole and a Hungarian come to me and a Slovak man come to me, a Frenchman next to me tells me: "What, you've already stayed, those of the Visegrad Group?" and it can be seen that it doesn't make it funny."
In the future, the EU, according to Vicenovás, is being awaited by tough new budgetary policy rules for the Member States.
It is perceived as a very real thing of the two-speed concept, if they are not more, in Europe, rather than the "European Federation."
"We've got countries in the Eurozone and countries outside the Eurozone."
"We have the Schengen area and we also have the enhanced co-operation procedure."
"It was said for the first time that only the countries they want and those who don't have to stay out."
"These procedures will become more and more and they will strengthen the two-speed Europe," Vicenova is expected.
In this regard, he also highlighted the role of the Frish and Herman van Rompuy in the application of the principle, according to which all issues affecting the entire EU should be discussed among the Member States, not only among the members of the Eurozone.
- Brand story:
The innovative IBM kick-start its second century of existence
If someone were to look for a term that defines IBM's strategy with great precision, the closest one would be "long-term."
For the general public, IBM is the symbol of a technological revolution, a company that is perceived to be current and yet a hundred years have passed since its founding.
The foundation of this long-running character was placed by the firm &apos; s first boss, visionary Thomas J. Watson Sr.
Already he clinched the motto that many problems in the world have a solution if people are willing to think.
His motto "Think" became his seal, around which the life of the giant future will be built (it was also the title of an internal magazine that IBM began publishing in 1935).
Watson was also the basis of a carefully erected enterprise culture.
"For us, IBM implies long-term thinking, a continuous movement towards the future.
IBM has survived and thrived for a hundred years for that reason, because it remained faithful to its basic principles, but without fear of the changes around it.
This has allowed us, during our first century, to transform technology, business and society.
We hope to thrive even more in our second century," said the current company director Samuel J. Palmisano during his anniversary.
Sophisticated system
Watson, already in the 1930s, was concerned with the care of his or her employees such as the businessman of Baa in the Czech Republic (IBM employees were one of the first U.S. operatives who enjoyed paid, secure vacations, elegant uniforms and a sophisticated remuneration system that rewarded the best inventors and sellers.
The same long-term sophistication is maintained by IBM in the relationship with the customer.
Already from the very beginning the company was able to discover talents (because it was one of the first companies in the world to end any kind of discrimination, it could choose seamlessly).
In line with the anti-discrimination strategy is IBM &apos; s current plan, whereby Palmisano will be replaced next year by a woman: Virginia Rometty.
She's already been working for 30 years at IBM, where she's been head of sales, marquetin and strategies.
Over the last few years IBM has participated in the so-called virtual encounters, rain of online ideas, from which infinities of future strategies and innovations arise.
Unsurprisingly, there are ideas, because almost half a million people work in the firm.
An institution with a good reputment
The fixed position in the business provides the company with a strong indoor and external image.
"From the very beginning, IBM maintained an institution concept rather than tech company," says Harvard Business School's chief economist and author of the book on IBM Rosabeth Moss Kanter.
"IBM isn't a tech company, it's a tech company that's trying to solve business problems for tech users," says George Colony of consultancy Forrester Research.
As the company itself says there's a difference between getting into the business and directly creating a business.
IBM - or the "Great Green" - as it is known in the U.S. familiar because of its logo, has in the last hundred years become one of the world's largest companies.
It's arguably the most versatile tech company (develops both equipment and programs, infrastructures and web hosting services within a wide range of services, ranging from servers to nanotechnology).
It has the largest number of patents of all U.S. technology companies and five of its employees have received the Nobel Prize.
Its inventions include the ATM, payment cards, financial swaps, barcodes and diskettes.
IBM will bill $220 billion this year. This year it has become the second trusting trusting firm (the first is Apple), when it first surpassed Microsoft in 1996.
Looking to the future
It'sn't all products and innovations have just satisfied the taste of the customers and the moment (IBM didn't bet on its own operating system, OS/2, Prodigy online services or IBM PCjr computers).
"If your business is based on moving forward, you can't anchored emotionally in the past," said Samuel J. Palmisano.
For this reason, IBM has no problems building its bases on other platforms (and likewise, if they don't work, it abandons them).
And that's the difference of the competition, Microsoft, which basically keeps and drops exclusively with Windows.
For future technology
When it comes to sales, IBM focuses mainly on services, of which approximately a fifth are from programs and a fifth of equipment.
However, even in 1990, the teams accounted for more than half the share of suppliers.
Since 2000, IBM has sold low-yielding assets totalling $15-billion. During the same period it has invested $58-billion in the purchase of future technology.
Investments have continued since 2008, when the modern global financial crisis was first manifested.
IBM performs according to the premises of its first boss, Thomas J. Watson, who during the great economic crisis of the 29-year-billion increased investments.
It seems that so far the company IBM has been able to weather the crisis.
The value of IBM's shares since the beginning of 2008 has increased stratospherically to 120%, the company's net profit has continued to increase.
IBM's currency is to change everything, except its value.
You can therefore hope that when the next crisis comes, this flexible gigant will endure.
IBM Story: From the Cheese Clider to Space Flight
IBM (International Business Machines) was founded in New York a hundred years ago, being its original name Computing Tabulating Recording Corporation.
The IBM name was already adopted in 1924 - it was originally a brand used in branches of Canada and South America.
The basis that gave rise to the birth of IBM arises from the merger of four companies dating back to the 1880s.
It possessed inventions such as the famous "shocking clock," which allows you to control the entry and exit of the employees.
The architect of the new company was businessman Charles Ranlett Flint, who until 1930 also presided over his supervisory board.
Flint scratched the boss of a competition company, Thomas J. Watson, to help him with the management of the company.
The new leader eclipsed the founder of IBM (Willion's ideas continue to be the steadfast basis on which corporate culture and identity are based).
Watson ran the company until 1952.
IBM, as its name indicates, began with the production and sale of all kinds of business appliances, from meat and cheese cutters to cash registers.
During the first four years Watson ran the company, turnover doubled to $9 million and the company's large expansion began across continents.
Watson was so crucial for the company that, almost after forty years occupying the boss's seat, he was replaced by his son, Thomas Watson Junior.
Under his leadership, IBM began to work on the development of artificial intelligence and developed the first programming language.
In the 1960s IBM's personnel and computers helped NASA to guide flights into space (to Mercury, Saturn and in 1969 the famed flight to the Moon).
Meanwhile, in 1964 IBM developed the first generation of computers, the IBM System/360 series.
The classic "peaces," which established the standard of computers at the same time, came to the market under the name IBM 5150 in 1981.
Twenty years later, IBM sold its production of computers to the Chinese company Lenovo, which, meanwhile, acquired the consulting part of the Pricewaterhouse Coopers company and began offering a complete high-value-added advice and services.
IBM's strategy over the last three years is the Smarter Planet concept, that is, "a smarter planet."
Its objective is to make more rational use of the technology, although the areas have not yet been planned and the circumstances have not been improved, for example with regard to the transport and administration of cities.
For the business sector, IBM presented not long ago the "Smart Commerce" platform that allows retailers to manage the entire trading cycle.
IBM has a long history in this country.
Already in 1932 he opened a branch in Czechoslovakia at that time, the first country that had it in Central and Eastern Europe.
Today, the IBM ER also operates the strategic outsourcing center in Brno.
The headquarters for Central and Eastern Europe is located in Prague. Also here there is a world-renowned research team that works with voice recognition.
