How travel prices have evolved since 1989
Until 1989 there were only five authoritated state travel agencies in the Czechoslovakia at the time: Eedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
Journey agencies at the time were in most cases selling trips only to the socialist bloc, as trips to the West were very restrained.
Czech tourists therefore went on vacation to the beaches of the GDR (German Democratic Republic), to the Soviet Union (Sochi in Crimea in the Black Sea) to Bulgaria and Romania (both in the Black Sea) and to Hungary (Balaton).
A positive exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when you could travel to Yugoslavia, however the Yugoslav regime then leaned to the west and the trips of Czech tourists were then quite restrained.
A great paradox of socialism was the fact that the citizens of the socialist bloc (i.e. also the Czechoslovaks) were not allowed to travel freely and individually even through the socialist countries.
If a citizen wanted to travel individually, such as Poland or the Soviet Union, he needed a letter of invitation, otherwise the individual journey was not possible.
The travel agencies also offered trips to the West at that time, but one had to queue with a sack of bed and spend all the night, or have "good acquaintances" at the agency.
Even so it wasn't all won.
The so-called "promise of change" that was the condition for trips to the West wasn't conjudged the "unreliable politically unreliable people," who thus couldn't travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely convertible currency and, without a guarantee of exchange, it was not possible to obtain the resources (the western currency) to travel to the West.
The vast majority of Czechs spent their vacations in their own country, so there were a large number of huts and huts and summer houses that the Czechs acquired. This custom still persists.
Since 1989, a genuine revolution has undergone a real revolution since 1989.
While up to 1989 for us it would no longer spend the vacations in the GDR or Bulgaria, last year the Czechs made 4.5 million vacation trips to countries around the world.
It is a great paradox, of which we are no longer unasslecence today, the fact that the currently favorite destination, Croatia, until 1989 it was not possible to travel freely, because it was part of the Yugoslavia at that time.
At the beginning of the 1990s the visa waiver was imposed for travel to the neighbouring states of Western Europe, prompting a huge wave of short-term visits, especially to Vienna or Germany.
For a long time, the Czechoslovak didn't look fed up with the excursions in the West, everyone wanted to see him at least for a moment.
For example, groups of Czechs slept in cottages in tent parks slept, as our income at the time didn't allow us to pay for a hotel or pension.
A typical characteristic of the Czechs of that time was to take with them the food (conserves, sausages, etc.) to save.
Another important moment was the introduction of the convertibility of the Czech krone, so our tourists could finally, after decades, freely buy Western currency.
Our standard of living was also increasing the average wages, which around 1997 resulted in an increase in the demand for travel.
With the increase in demand for travel the agencies began, from 1997 regularly, to prepare trips using charter flight connections, which made travel prices incredibly low compared to the past, thanks to the possibility of obtaining discounts for the volume of passengers.
At the end of the 90s of the last century, the Czechs already travelled without a visa to almost every developed country except the U.S., which further simplified our trips.
Another interesting moment in the history of post-revolutionary tourism took place already in 2000, when it was organized, thanks to enormous demand, the first direct flight from the HRC to an exotic country, and went to Thailand.
As of that year, direct charter flights depart every year with Czech tourists towards distant and hot countries. This winter it will fly directly from the Czech Republic to 12 exotic destinies.
Exotic countries are currently visited by about 100,000 Czechs a year.
Another interesting moment of this time, which symbolizes our post-revolutionary development, arrived in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S. eliminated visas for Czech tourists, leaving our country to be among the most developed countries in the world.
In 2008 the Czechs, for the first time in their history, have spent more than half of their vacations abroad (50.3 per cent) and for the first time in their history have been spent on trips abroad more than 15,000 Czech kronor per head, including expenses at the stay site.
Convenated Ordinary General Assembly to elect the president, who could end again in a stalemate.
For almost five months, Czech soccer has been without a president and it is likely that the successor of Ivan Haek as President of the Czech Football Federation (FAR), will not be known even after the convening of the next Extraordinary General Assembly that will take place this Thursday in Nymburk.
In fact, there has not yet been an agreement on the voting rules to be followed.
Without them, you won't even be able to vote to choose between the three candidates.
Those interested in presiding over Czech soccer are Jindsich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomaá Paclík.
Hasek resigned from his post in June not without first summoning his colleagues for the purpose of finding a strong person who knew how to take care of football in the future.
However, after the conclusion of the Haek era, a controversy broke out between the two federations of the association unable to reach an agreement to choose a common candidate.
This became evident on September 16 when the new president had to be elected.
The delegates of the General Assembly gathered in vain at the Casa Nacional (Národní d'm), located in the Praguense neighborhood of Smív, because the talks had been blocked and not only in terms of the method of election.
There was no consensus either as to who in the General Assembly had the right to vote - only the legal representatives or also delegates with full powers.
From that moment on and because of this only point, there was a partial agreement, also supported by the recommendation of the Institute of State and Law, according to which representation by full powers is not possible.
However, disputes over the electoral code are still open.
More glimpse the situation could no longer shed the Executive Committee which, in its regular or extraordinary meetings, it basically didn't solve anything.
The opinion on the possibility of a vote has already changed a couple of times already, but the nerve point remains the same.
The question is as follows: how to proceed in the third round to avoid stalemate?
"The dispute revolves around whether it should proceed in accordance with the statutes or find another model," says Duan Svoboda, one of the three vice-presidents of the FA-R, who, from the management of the association, represents professional football.
According to the model proposed in the statutes, in the third round the candidate who has the highest percentage of support in the federation to which he will present his candidacy will win.
The Moravian Region is ready to reach a commitment so that it can win that candidate with a higher total percentage of votes in both federations.
The bohemian party, however, imposes the condition that the winner, according to these percentage calculations, must have a majority of at least two-thirds.
"That, however, would remove the meaning from the previous two rounds and completely deny the concept of a bicameral system," this is how Svoboda understands the objections of the Moravian side, according to which it would be best not to touch the order and to vote according to the applicable statutes.
Astonishingly, he also agrees with Toma Paclík, although it is assumed that the electoral regulations established by the statutes of all the nominees are better suited to Celtic.
"I'd like to see the maximum vote in accordance with the statutes".
"So they don't invent any nonsense about the representatives like the last General Assembly," says the owner of Victoria Plzen, who is running for the first time as a nominee and directs his remarks mainly against Pelta.
Pelta is still optimistic.
According to him, in Nymburk it will be decided about the new president and he believes the future candidates will cooperate constructively.
"Either the passions will appease with our national team's match at the European Championship today.
"Football will again be a protagonist, and this will cooperate more comfortably," says Jablonice's representative, who of the three candidates has more experience in football management, both at the professional and regional levels.
Rajchl is the most renticent.
"I hope an agreement will be reached, but unfortunately the situation is much more complicated."
"It's not about bohemian-whistle conflict, but there's attempts to twist the rules to your own convenience."
"That may result in a new blockade of the General Assembly."
"Either way, I hope that common sense will be imposed," Rajchl said.
There's not much more time left for all sides to come to an agreement.
Some negotiations had to take place in Montenegro, but who can know until the game begins if the soccer team will be complete?
The special aircraft, in which they had to travel to Podgorica, among other Vice Presidents Dalibor Kucera and Rajchl, did not leave this morning due to technical problems in Prague, having to look for an alternative to transport part of the executive committee to the place where the qualifying match will take place.
Perhaps the decisive meeting, at last, could take place at the ministerial level, to be held this Wednesday.
Josef Dobe , Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, would very much like to incorporate the divided parties into the most powerful sports organization in the country so that an agreement can be reached.
There is a lot at stake: state subsidies, the credibility of soccer and also the upcoming General Assembly of the Central Committee of the Czechoslovak sports federation (STV), which will try to provide a new direction to Czech sport following the financial problems of the Sazka lottery house.
Paclík will struggle to reach the presidency, because he doesn't find a more suitable candidate.
If the head of Pilsen's soccer players, Tomas Paclik, before the Extraordinary General Assembly knew the name of a suitable candidate who satisfied all his ideas about the new president of the Czech Soccer Federation (FAR), he would not stand as a candidate for such a relevant position.
"If there was a suitable person, I'd be battling for it and I wouldn't run for the election."
"But to this day it hasn't been possible to find such a person," said Paclík for the Czech News Agency
What motivates Paclík to enter the challenge with Miroslav Pelta and Henry Rajchl is exactly the possibility of blocking Pelta, the presumptive winner of the elections, which paves the way for the presidency of the Czech Football Federation (FAR).
Paclík also points out that, if he expires, he would remain in the position of president only until 2013, when the turn of the ordinary general assembly would arrive.
In that position he would like to see an pundit and good manager who isn't too related to football events.
"I've already talked to a few people I could take into account".
"It was crazy for me to hear some say they don't even want to identify with Czech soccer at the moment."
"I would like to change that vision," he adds.
What presumably annoys Paclík at the moment is Pelta's relationship with Pilsen Regional Soccer Association President Roman Berbr, a man with a controversial past, known outside the world as a powerful lobist with the ability to influence the vote of delegates at the general assembly.
For Paclík it is so unacceptable to see Pelta sitting in the presidential armchair.
"If he wins, he'll keep the status in place and swimming in soccer will change."
"With the support of Berbra, an ambitious businessman, soccer will make even less profit," Paclík said.
It also mentions the fact that a few years ago Pelta's name was associated with a wiretapping during the investigation of a corruption case, of which Pelta went unpunished.
"I don't know what prestige soccer could bring Peltic's victory."
"And with what eyes UEFA and FIFA will look at us," said Paclík, who also cautiously takes his previous statement "woulding the Pilsen Club if Pelta becomes president."
In the event of winning Thursday's elections, Paclík could consider co-operation with Pelta in the future.
Of the same opinion it is the other candidate, Rajchl, who has been supported by Paclík before the General Assembly in September that ended up in a fiasco.
"He (Peltic) would be the first to follow."
"She's very capable".
"You could imagine, for example, as head of representation".
"but don't as an association manager".
"I don't stick our noses where I don't feel capable either," added Paclík, who in September attracted much attention for supporting the suspension of the Arbitrast Commission chaired by Ludek Macel, later replaced by Dagmar Damkova.
Paclík admits that he would dare to manage the association.
He resorts to his management capacities for private companies, since since last summer he is also the Director of the Pilsen, who with his support celebrated the first division triumph in May.
"I've got the strength to change a few things in the field of football and contribute to their safe practice."
"In order to reduce the influence of some lobbyists, they probably tend to influence the decisions of the Disciplinary and Appeals Committee, producing unpleasant situations," Paclík said without hiding his antipathies towards Sparta President Daniel Koetínsk.
One of the priorities would also be to consolidate the relationship between the FA&R and the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which it exerted pressure on political leaders to allocate more money to lottery-imposed sports.
"That's one of the key problems that the new president should dedicate himself to."
"If gambling taxes were to be increased, as the political parties announced, I would support their arguments, but, frankly, they have to tell us where all this money is going to stop," he says.
In addition, Paclík now does not even take into account his association with the Pilsner Club, although he himself claims that the president of the FAR should be "non-partisan."
Peleta is in turn associated with Jablonec and Rajchl with Dukla of Prague.
"It's not ideal".
"I don't see any sense of hypocritically ceding my Pilsen Club actions to someone else and then coming back there after a year and a half," Paclík said.
Pelta promised the FAR new statutes that would put an end to the stalemate of the elections.
Two days before the General Assembly of the Football Association one of the candidates for the presidency, Miroslav Pelt, promised that, if he were elected, he would ensure that the FAR had new statutes that would definitely serve to resolve, in the future, disputes over the electoral regulations.
The Jablonec representative is optimistic about this Thursday's meeting in Nymburk, and believes that the pre-election conflict could move towards a solution today with the possible victory of the soccer team.
Already before the elections not held in September, Pelta promised to focus, should he be elected, precisely on representation and fundraiser.
He decided to participate at the last minute in the last general assembly, so this two-month postponement could go well to defend and present, in a broader context, his ideas on the management of soccer.
"People at all levels take care of soccer for a long time now and they expect to meet the new president this Thursday."
"They feel it's been the time for someone to manage the association," thinks the man who is mainly associated with the Jablonice Club, but who worked at the Sparta and the Association's executive committee.
For this reason, he believes he has enough experience to manage Czech football.
He is convinced that behind the current problems with the elections is the euphoria that reigned at the time of Ivan Heek.
"Unfortunately, at the General Assembly of June the statutes that weren't as perfect as they all thought."
"If we exclude certain interpretations, we already have a president since September," Pelta warned.
Exactly the readaptation of the most important document of the Civic Association was used as a decoy to beat the delegates.
"If I'm elected president, I'll take it as a personal challenge and I'm willing to respond in case this fails."
"It's a matter of principles, without which we can't exist," says Pelta.
Also, in theory, he tries not to mess with his opponents.
Already in the past he had warned that he saw himself collaborating with Henry Rajchl, whom he respects for his training in law, being aware that his opponent would also be his first vice-president.
Thomas Paclík hadn't been attacked for a long time, who, unlike Pelta, didn't leave puppet with his head.
"He's spent more than a year in the soccer world, and he's already achieved a few successes."
"His attacks but they were embarrassing and a lot of people were stunned by the election campaign."
"That's why many of them hesitate about his true character," he told his opponent Pelta, who remains faithful to the statements already announced in September.
His most powerful weapon is, according to him, his knowledge of soccer, from the lowest competitions to the top.
The latter should be a promotional showcase for football.
"I'd also try to solve all the scandals that arose because of the Bohemians in the wake of last year's corruption scandal."
