Casa 21 in Vienna
The ward transferred
By Ingeborg Wiensowski
An architectural tale with ups and downs and a Happy End, the pavilion of the 1958 Austrian Universal Exhibition won awards, was in Vienna for a museum, remained empty and crumbled.
It's now reopened as an exhibition house.
Moster post-gerra architecture passionates, the clear shapes, glass halls, brave curls and new construction materials, typical of thought about technical progress, for which the Austrian pavilion of the architect Karl Schwanzer for the Universal exhibition of Brussels of 1958 is a suitable and well-known example.
The Schwanzer Pavilion then obtained the Architecture Grand Prix for its light and floating construction of steel and glass.
To this day the construction is considered a contemporary milestone of architecture.
He made such a well-known architect that he also built the pavilion of Austria in the following universal exhibition.
With its legendary four-cylinder tower for MBW plus the adjoining flattened building in Munich, it became internationally known.
Its pavilion, designed as a temporary construction for exhibitions, was erected after the universal exhibition at the Schweizer Garten next to South Vienna station, was renovated in 1962 and a 20th-century museum was inaugurated, it was dubbed the "home 20."
The building was suitable but lacked the proper spaces and technology for a museum; with no wall to hang art, not a good atmosphere.
In spite of this it was used until contemporary art in Vienna had a new home.
The building's been emptied since 2001.
At present, architect Adolf Krischanitz renovated the pavilion, he now calls it "house 21" and will be used in the future as a museum of contemporary art.
On Tuesday afternoon the belvedere museum was inaugurated, to which the house belongs, with the first exhibition, a stroke of luck in a certain way.
Agnes Husslein-Arco, the new director of the Austrian belvedere gallery since 2007, reached a fresh start.
Husslein-Arco, Ph.D. in art history with a considerable career, among other things ice skating in his youth, later in Sotheby's, the Guggenheim museum and founding director of the Salzburg Museum of Modern Art, carried out the renovations with the insistence of politicians and protectors of the monuments, provided public funds and private sponsors and established their use.
With the exception of contemporary art, the sculptor Fritz Wotruba's sculpturator of the eponymous foundation is exhibited, there should be a cafeteria and a bookstore and an office building will also be built.
Strict control, low budget
Krischanitz was the best choice to do so.
Not only because he significantly renovated the secession of Joseph Olbrichs in Vienna, he widened it and he was awarded the contest, if not because he studied with Schwanzwer at the Technical University of Vienna and the pavilion was an "important and immense place for him," in which he contemplated "almost every weekend" the exhibitions.
He was aware of the building's project and problem, which disappeared with his transfer to Vienna in its change of use with generosity and ease.
There's major problems with the current reforms, the building was "an energy devour," Krischanitz said.
You changed the profile of the windows of the glass facade, installed an insulating crystal reinforced with fiberglass and the glass ceiling was replaced with a special safety glass.
Thermal bridges and steel beams were reinforced or replaced and the asbestos ceilings were rehabilitated.
Two basements were used for the new use, a light panel descended between the house and the street provides the illumination, a bridge serves to enter the almost new building, but the Schwanzer pavilion.
Remncipal outdoor gates remain, the house is painted in mahogany-protected shades of oxidation.
It's exactly the way it used to be.
And all of this with strict control by the memorial office and a low-budget.
"I often had to find material that was good, inexpensive and also liked the memorial protection office," Krischanitz says and warns of rough ground in the basement.
In spite of all the pressures, problems and renovations, the building stays flexible and light.
"Trussels that came from Brussels has been tough," Krischanitz, a typical "casual appliance in the city's history," recently claimed.
Such an acquite is "always in the wrong place" and that is why he wanted to "awaken the right consciousness."
In this case the house 21 is not in the wrong place, there has been a stroke of urban luck. The completely new and planned "central station neighborhood," with houses and offices, is located next to Schweizer Garten.
And perhaps there's another luck blow: Casa 21 could receive a reinforcement of another Krischanitz construction, because collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berlin temporary art room and, as you can hear, negotiates with the city on a neighboring place in the Schweizer Garten.
