The support workers" union will sue the city for the layoffs
The syndicate that represents hundreds of school support workers who lost their jobs last month planned to sue the city on Wednesday, claiming the layoffs were unnecessary and discriminatory because of its disproportionate impact on schools catering to poor students.
The lawsuit is arguably the latest weapon available to the 37-year-old municipal district in its effort to revoke the layoffs.
Six-hundred-hundreds and two aides at the school, parental coordinators and other employees who were among the city's lowest-payers lost their jobs on October 7, in a move that union leaders have described as political revenge for their refusal to allow the city's access to a health-care fund run by working groups to close its budget gap last spring.
The municipal authorities have categorically denied the accusation.
At a town hall hearing last month, education adviser Dennis M. Walcott said the layoffs were part of the budget approved in June.
A council member counter-attacked, telling him that nothing in the budget specified that the school's support staff would be sacked.
Municipal district officials said they filed the complaint in writing, warning the Department of Education he would be sued. A press conference will be held on Wednesday to announce the official filing of the lawsuit in Manhattan State's Supreme Court.
The lawsuit will include Mr. Walcott and the city's Department of Education as defendants, and has eight workers dismissed as plaintiffs, all black or Latino women, as well as the majority of attendees who lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's legal department, said the city had not seen the lawsuit and was therefore unable to comment on its specific allegations.
According to a draft lawsuit obtained by The New York Times, the syndicate has focused on the number of layoffs in schools in poor areas of the city to support a claim for unfair treatment, saying that poor schools lost more attendees because they had been chronically subsidised.
It is noted that while there were no layoffs on Staten Island and a few layoffs in schools on the Upper East Side or TriBeCa, 17 school attendees lost their jobs in District 23 in Brownsville, Brooklyn, and 46 were fired in Districts 8 and 9 in the Southern Bronx.
Taking into account the loss of five of the eight attendees employed by Public School 36 in Harlem, where 68 per cent of students live in poverty, the lawsuit says, "This isn't a neighborhood of wealthy parents who can raise funds to cover the budget deficit."
P.D. The 36-degreement budget fell by 3.26 per cent this year, also losing money when the city shifted the way it calculates additional funding for every student per poor student in schools.
The syndicate also says high school principals may have been fooled by language in a memo that authorizes the sacking of parental co-ordinators.
The memo used the word "excessiveness" to describe the action, even though "excessively" more often describes the act of moving a teacher off the payroll of a school to a labour exchange, whose salaries are paid by the head office.
"Senses and six parental co-ordinators were fired," the draft complaint says, "and not just in surplus."
Finished mission, congress prepared to reach an agreement on Libya
It seems Republicans and Democrats on Capitol Hill are poised to agree on something related to the uprising in Libya.
Everything that was needed was for the rebellion to succeed, that veteran dictator Muamar Gaddafi would be captured and killed, and U.S. participation came to an end.
On Tuesday, the Senate Foreign Affairs Committee will consider a resolution, sponsored by President John Kerry (Democracy of Massachusetts), along with Sens. John McCain (Republican for Arizona) and Joseph I. Lieberman (independent of Connecticut), applauding the Libyans for their successful rebellion and U.S. troops for their "bravery."
"This resolution is to honor the braves of Libya, as they begin to rebuilt their country," Kerry said.
"It's a statement of bipartisan support for your democratic aspirations."
Language may seem harmless, but swimming about the debate on Libya has been easy so far.
This summer, lawmakers from both parties scrambling to each other and with the White House over the drafting of a series of resolutions to approve or reject the U.S. military's role in the U.S. mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain tabled a resolution authorizing the limited use of U.S. military forces in Libya.
The bill was defeated in the House by a wide bipartisan margin, which was never voted on in the Senate.
But on the same day the bill was scrapped in the House, the chamber also rejected a measure that would have cut funds for U.S. operations in Libya.
That left the U.S. mission in a kind of legislative purgatory - with Congress annoying enough to criticize Obama's handling of the mission, but not so angry as to actually skewench the grass under his feet.
The congressional indecision was then surpassed by events.
Tripoli fell into the hands of the Libyan rebels in August and Gaddafi was assassinated on October 20.
The NATO mission was officially completed on October 31.
The Senate could now be ready to act.
The resolution of the Foreign Affairs Committee "congratulates the people of Libya for their tremendous courage and extraordinary resistance to liberating themselves" and "gives the men and women of the U.S. Armed Forces and their coalition partners who participated in military operations to protect the people of Libya for their extraordinary bravery and professionalism."
The resolution also "declares U.S. national interest in a successful and irreversible transition to democracy in Libya."
Will the new measure ensure the full support of the Foreign Affairs Committee, or will it divide the lawmakers as the June bill did?
The latest version was rejected by Senator Richard G. Locence (Indiana), the committee's top Republican.
Lugar spokesman said on Monday that he had not yet seen the final version of the new bill.
On the other side of the Capitol, the House will vote this week on a constitutional amendment demanding a balanced budget, a legislative priority for almost all Republican congressmen and some Democrats.
The measure, sponsored by Republican Bob Goodlatte (Republican by Virginia), will be the first constitutional amendment to the budget or any other issue that in fact achieves an assembly-wide vote in the House or Senate in this Congress.
Other lawmakers have so far not been successful in bringing their amendments to the assembly, but not because of a lack of tries.
Goodlatte's is just one of the 68 constitutional amendments that have been introduced in this Congress, covering a wide range of issues.
Some are duplicative - the same bill is proposed in both the House and the Senate - and others with slight variations on each other.
There's at least 15 versions of the Budget Balance amendment in the House alone, and another handful that "will control federal spending."
Some of these bills are also identical to each other, but they give their sponsors - especially rookie Republicans looking to refine their resumes - the opportunity to boast that they were the "authors" of that measure.
The Budget Control Act, which was passed in August, required both the House and the Senate to vote on an amendment to the budget balance.
In March, 58 senators voted in favor of a non-binding resolution that supports the idea of such an amendment.
Beyond the budget, several members want to limit the number of mandates that members of Congress can fulfil, while Rep. José E. Serrano (defeated for New York) wants to remove the two-term limit for the presidents.
Rep. Jesse L. Jackson Jr. (Democratic for Illinois) has proposed a series of amendments that guarantee the right to quality education and health care, among other issues.
An amendment banning same-sex marriage is a perennial proposition, as well as one that prohibits the burning of flags.
Some lawmakers also want to allow annulment of federal laws if they are rejected by two-thirds of the states.
So enthusiastic that they have been the members in proposing amendments, they are not moving at a faster rate than in previous years.
In the 111th congress, the legislators proposed 77 amendments, and submitted 66 in the 110th.
The rhythm is unanecceptable compared to that of the 1990s when members regularly submitted more than 150 amendments every two years, according to figures provided by the Library of the Senate.
But they didn't have much more success then than they have now.
The last amendment that was added - on the 27 - was ratified in 1992, but in fact it was proposed by Congress in 1789.
