The Walls Have Eyes: How Investigators Are Studying You on Facebook
Before he became the far-left front in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was just another guy who adorned his anti-immigration views online.
On Monday, Breivik, who admitted to carrying out a massacre in Norway in July, which left 77 dead, faced his first public court hearing.
Although Breivik may have acted alone, he was far from being alone in cyberspace: He had spent most of the time that preceded his attack in front of his computer, chatting to some of the millions of nationalists who support right-wing groups on social media.
After this summer's tragedy, the researchers wanted to know more about these people.
But how do you find them?
Easy - you've just gotta get on Facebook.
"We realised that it wasn't so hard to get to them," says Jamie Bartlett, lead author of a recently published report on European digital populism by the British think tank DEMOS.
Facebook's stash of personal information is so encyclopaedic, Bartlett says that researchers could simply use the advertising tool to determine their desired demographic characteristics with scientific accuracy - the way sellers have been using for years.
Bartlett's team found half a million fans of right-wing groups across Europe and then directed ads, but instead linking them to a new band or diet product, the adverts invited users to complete a survey that asked about their level of education, attitudes towards violence and optimism about their own future.
Upon arriving via Facebook, Bartlett and his colleagues were able to study more than 10,000 followers from 14 far-right parties in 11 European countries - all without leaving the office.
"It's an entirely new way to do an investigation," says Bartlett.
Some of the results aren't entirely astonishable: Online supporters of right-wing groups tend to be young, male and angry about immigration.
However, in a sort of twist, "those who combine their online activism with out-of-the-line activism are more reasonable, more democratic and less violent than those left behind the computer screen."
DEMOS' work is just an example of how Facebook is becoming an important new tool in the hands of scientists.
Research centers, medical researchers and political scientists are using the site to look at everything from health problems to social trends as expressed in "Like," messages on walls and status updates.
With more than 800 million active users adding an average of three pieces of content a day, Facebook's "data supernova" is generating a boom in research, boosting the number of academic jobs with the site's name on the title to almost 80 per cent over the past five years.
For some researchers, the beauty of Facebook is that it allows you to study the people you couldn't get close to with a folder on the street.
"Before, when you were trying to survey people from the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify."
"You'd have to go through the game, and they'd never allow you access," says Bartlett.
"Facebook eliminates all that - you're going to them directly."
Others say the site could provide a way to identify and address health-social problems.
A recent study carried out by Dr. Megan Moreno of the University of Wisconsin-Madison and her colleagues found that university students who talked about their drunken feats on Facebook had a significantly higher risk of drinking problems than students who remained silent on the subject.
Moreno suggests that student classmates, such as residential counsellors, could control the site and intervene to help a student who posts too many status updates with their drunks.
"You couldn't deal with a problem if you couldn't be diagnosed," said Dimitri Christakis, Moreno's co-author and Director of the Center for Health, Behavior and Child Development at Seattle's Children's Research Institute, told the Washington Post.
"We've found a way to identify at-risk children who wouldn't otherwise be diagnosed."
Facebook is also constantly "punctuating" its own population to obtain data.
The site's Data Team has produced statistics on relationship status and Valentine &apos; s Day, expectation of voter turnout in the middle of the legislative period in 2010, and a National Happiness Index (mainly using the information that can be accessed - long-standing digital freedom advocates who argue that the site should make its large amount of research data available to others).
Facebook users, however, know that the site is watching them, whether they like it or don't - the sacrifice for playing Scrabble with a friend thousands of kilometers away is to allow the site to mine your personal information.
But what's happening when Facebook users become part of a survey they don't even know?
While the researchers have well-defined guidelines on how to collect their off-the-shelgree data, online it's a free bar for everyone.
"I don't think that many of the users have even thought about the fact that a researcher might be examining their profiles," says Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at London's Knowledge Lab, about a 2006 study he carried out with Facebook's public student walls.
"As far as they were concerned, it was only between them and their friends."
Then there's the question of the methodology.
Even off the line, there's no guarantee that an investigative issue is being completely honest.
On Facebook, it's impossible to know that some of a user's profile information and wall messaging are true.
"What you're saying on Facebook and what you're doing off-Face is two completely different things," says Selwyn.
That is why, despite his years as a tech researcher, Selwyn believes a pelkey straddle and pen are still the best investigative tools anyone can use.
"There's no surrogate to get into the real world and talk to real people," he says.
"Social research is supposed to be about the social - and a lot of the social is still happening offline."
"Frehetic and inhuman: Anders Behring Breivik makes his first public appearance in court
Anders Behring Breivik, the man who confessed to be the mastermind of the two attacks that killed 77 people in Norway this summer, has described himself as a "resistance fighter" during his first public appearance in the Oslo court.
"I'm a military commander in the Norwegian resistance movement and in the Knights Templar of Norway," he said in front of a room filled with 500 people.
"I am opposed to the court, as he received the mandate from organizations that support the ideology of hatred and because he supports multiculturalism."
"I recognize the facts but I don't plead guilty."
Judge Torkjel Nessheim interrupted Breivik during that monologue because he "didn't want to give Breivik the opportunity to use this hearing as a platform to express his views."
For the same reason he refused to allow Breivik, who brought a written speech in advance, to address the relatives of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also ordered the police to keep Breivik in custody for another 12 weeks, banned him from accessing the media for four weeks, and said the authorities would have to have strict control over all visits and correspondence for eight weeks.
Around 30 survivivors and relatives of the victims attended the hearing.
Some came hoping to look Breivik in the eye, others to confirm he is gently locked.
They all attended with the aim of moving towards the conclusion.
"I thought it seemed cold and inhumane," a survivor from Utoya told Norwegian broadcaster NRK.
"It was uncomfortable, but for me it was a step forward after seeing and hearing the suspect."
Dressed in a dark outfit and blue tie, Breivik stayed calm and professional throughout the audience and watched reporters and survivivor in the eye as he walked in and out of the building.
Despite Breivik's bizarre notion of being part of a major "resistance" movement, the judge determined that he is not a mentally ill person, saying there's no evidence he acted with accomplices.
It's possible for some that the July 22-on-July attacks appear to be the work of an extremist rather than a bunch of radicals.
But that doesn't automatically soften the grief and turmoil an entire nation still feels.
"I'd like him to look like a monster, but he doesn't think so," said a relative of a victim.
"It'd be a lot easier if it looked."
7 Lessons From Steve Jobs' Lost Interview, shown in a theater this week
After the death of Steve Jobs on October 5, there were some who compared him to Henry Ford - distinguishing the genius of tech as the Great Modern Inventor.
But what Apple's fans might find the most revealing, in a newly rediscovered Jobs-disgraced interview he's stripped as a preview of a series of special screenings in major markets this week, is how much he understood this creative mind about the trade processes and workflows of the products.
He was certainly a self-recognised designer, dreamer and hippie, but he was also a meticulous organizer and a yonki of the flowcharts - a man who believed that many executives suffered "a disease consisting of thinking that a great idea is 90 per cent of the job... [but] there's a huge amount of work between a great idea and a great product."
In 1995, Bob Cringely was unveiling the television series The Triumph of the Nerds on the dawn of the personal computer, and sat for more than an hour with Jobs for an extensive and bizarre conversation.
At the time it had been barely 10 years since Jobs had been forced to leave Apple, and he was already working intensively on his new computer company NeXT, waiting for the popular adoption of the Internet.
While a small part of Cringely's interview was used in Nerds, he said the originals of the interview disappeared during his transport.
It was only after Jobs' death that a full copy of VHS was unveiled in the director &apos; s garage.
This slightly re-released archival material is the basis of Steve Jobs: The 68-minute lost interview, which arrives at the Landmark cinemas on Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone known for hating in-depth interviews, Jobs here seems surprisingly willing to expose his tech philosophies and business strategies.
It's very pensive.
At four different points throughout the interview, there's great silence between the questions and the answers - from 10 to 15 seconds of musing, where Jobs clearly weighs his thoughts, searching for precision.
There are also moments when Jobs" perfectionism comes to the surface, where he seems eager due to Cringely's questions, pushing him to go quicker towards the most important issues or arguments.
Pictured before the Pixar era, the iPod or the iPhone, there's something bizarrely prophesy in The Lost Interview, when Jobs assesses what he sees other companies do well and wrong, and offers his own vision for the future of computing.
It's one thing to read posthumous assessments of Jobs' career, but there's something raw and inspiring about the Jobs we see here, the eager dreamer on the brink of greatness.
He's got a glimpse of how things can and should be.
Not long after he explained everything in detail here, he went and did.
Fast-apsummer summary of seven notable sequences from The Lost Interview:
About how he learned to run a business:
"Throughout the years in the business, I've always asked, "Why do you do things?" and the answers they invariably give you are: "Oh, that's just the way it's done."
Nobody knows why he does what he's doing, in business nobody thinks about things very deeply.
That's what I've found.
Jobs takes to detail his efforts to simplify Apple's accounting.
Bewildered by the way it recorded the costs - often starting with a "standard couture" that was done to the eye of good bullshit, and which was then adjusted with a "variable" - he developed an automated factory that ensured they could determine commercial costs in detail.
On the joke to the Pope:
Jobs narrates the "Blue Case" he himself built with Steve Wozniak - a device that allowed people to effectively hack the phone company and make free long-distance calls.
It's been widely written about the device, but Job's euphoria here by describing the Blue Box as an act of empowerment is contagious.
It describes how he and Wozniak tested the box by using a public phone, making a call, and then connecting from an AT&T network to another, while creating a loop with as many satellites as possible.
"We were wrapping things around the world a half-dozen times and shouted through the pay-to-voiced phone and going back a minute later to the pay-to-door phone next door," says Jobs with chuckle.
"We were young and what we learned was that we could build something ourselves that could control billions of dollars in infrastructure around the world."
We both didn't know a lot, but we were able to build a small thing that I could control a giant thing - it was an incredible lesson, and I don't think Apple would ever have existed without it.
Jobs goes on to describe a glorious joke that he and Wozniak almost abandoned, calling the Vatican through the Blue Box in the middle of the night and asking to speak to the Pope, while doing his best imitation of Henry Kissinger.
As several members of the Catholic hierarchy were summoned in the middle of the night to speak to the U.S. diplomat, the two erupted in laughter just before the Pope himself was woken to go and answer the phone.
About falling in love with tech (and phone-selling with Bill Hewlett):
At the age of the age of 12, hunting for spare parts to build a frequency meter, he searched Bill Hewlett in the phone book and made a call.
Soon after his 20-minute phone conversation, Jobs got a part-time summer job at Hewlett-Packard.
"That's a significant influence on me, it was the only company I'd seen at that age and it formed my vision of what a company was and how well they treated its employees."
Jobs later became one of the HP employees who visited the company's research labs in Palo Alto, where he saw "the first desktop computer ever made."
It was as big as a suitcase, I had a small cathotic tube screen, and I fell in love with it.
I'd get a trip to HP as a teen and I'd lose my time with that machine and write programs for it.
On improvising innovation:
At various points in the interview, Jobs speaks about inventing new products on the fly.
Soon in his career, as he devoted himself to selling a handful of base plates, a customer asked if he could assemble the entire equipment.
Working with only 30 days of credit, I had to find out the assembly and delivery of the finished devices.
Later, while he was at Apple, he remembers the struggles he faced in the development of a computer mouse:
"I remember having dramatic arguments ... they yelled at me that it would take five years [to create a mouse that would cost $300], and I'd get tired and went away and found a designer.
Ninety days later, we had a mouse that we could build for $15 and that was totally reliable.
About large companies losing strength:
Long before he spearheaded Apple renaissance, Jobs prophetically previewed how many industry leaders would stumble in his vision, and lose control of their market share.
"Say that you're working on IBM or Xerox, so you do photocopiers and printers better, and what?"
You have a monopoly on market share, so the company has no more success.
The sales and marketing departments increase success, so it's these kinds of people who end up running the companies and the people in charge of the products are kicked out of the decision-making forum.
The genius of the products that led to this position of monopoly rots because of people who don't have the notion of good products and bad products - the knowledge of the necessary trade.
That's what's happened in Xerox...
Xerox could have taken over the entire computer industry.
It could've been ten times its size, it could've been Microsoft in the '90s...
They took the defeat of victory.
About innovation as an artistic expression:
"There's an enormous amount of work between a great idea and a great product ... as the great idea evolves, changes and grows."
You learn a lot about the subtleties of it.
There's sacrifices you've got to do - some things you cann't get them to do the electrons, the glass makes, the robots do it, the factories do.
You've got to keep 5,000 things in your brain - these concepts - adjust them all at the same time...
In the end it all comes down to taste - it comes down to trying to expose yourself to the best a human team has done, and then try to adapt those things you're doing.
Picasso said that the good artists copie, the great artists steal.
We've never been embarrassed to steal great ideas.
What in part made Macintosh great was that the people who were working on it were musicians, poets, artists, zoologists, historians who happened to be the best computer scientists in the world.
If it wasn't for computing, I might be doing wonderful things in other fields.
Scalia and Thomas dine on health-care law rivals as court accepts case
On the day the U.S. Supreme Court met behind closed doors to discuss the politically contentious question of whether they would accept defiance of President Obama's health-care law, two of its justices, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, were haunted at a dinner sponsored by the law firm that will present the case before the high court.
The occasion was last Thursday, when the nine judges met at a preliminary hearing to thoroughly study the requests for review.
One of the cases in question was a lawsuit filed by 26 states in defiance of the total health care reform approved by Congress over the past year, a law that has been a cry for conservative activists across the country.
The magistrates have agreed to accept the lawsuit and, in fact, a historic five-and-a-half-hour plea is awaited by March, and the result is likely to further mount the 2012 presidential race, which will be in full swing at the time when the court's decision is known.
The lawyer who will appear in court and plead that the law must be scrapped will probably be Paul Clement, who served as U.S. attorney-general during the George W. Bush administration.
Clement's law firm Bancroft PLLC was one of nearly two dozen companies that helped sponsor the Federalist Society's annual dinner, a group that's spent years defending conservative legal principles.
Another dinner-boosting firm, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations that defied the law, the National Federation of Independent Business.
Another sponsor was pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc., which has a huge financial interest in the outcome of the litigation.
Dinner took place at a hotel in Washington, hours after the court's pre-judgment hearing on the case.
Attendeees included, among others, Mitch McConnell, the most important Republican in the Senate and an outspoken opponent of the health-care law.
Special guests at dinner? Scalia and Thomas.
It's nothing new: The two judges have been attending Federalist Society events for years.
And it's not something that conflicts with ethical standards.
In fact, judges are exempt from the Code of Conduct that governs the actions of lower federal magistrates.
If they weren't, you'd say they'd fall under the Canon 4C Code, which says: "A judge can attend fund-raising events from law-related organizations and others, although the judge may not be a speaker, a guest of honour, or appear on such an event's agenda."
However, Scalia and Thomas' absolute proximity to two of the law firms in the case, as well as to a company with enormous financial interest, was enough to alarmed ethics activists in the government.
"This stunning breach of ethics and indifference to the code goes against the lawsuits of several justices that the court abides by the same rules that apply to all federal judges," said Bob Edgar, the president of Common Cause (Comminus Cause).
"Judgments were haunted at a gala fundraiser with lawyers who have pending cases in court."
His appearance and fundraiser attendance for this event undermines any demand for impartiality and is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas have shown little interest in critics who say they too easily mix the court's activity with an agenda driven by groups such as the Federalist Society.
And Thomas's wife, Ginni, is a leading conservative activist.
On the other hand, the Conservatives argue that it's Judge Elena Kagan, who has an ethical problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan served as attorney-general of the Obama administration when the first legal challenges to the law were presented in the lower court.
His critics have pushed for Kagan to refrain from seeing the case, saying she was too involved in defending the law to now be unfazed.
Kagan hasn't given any indication he's gonna do it.
Boeing Receives A Record Order of $18 Million
The Emirates airline is calling for 50 Boeing 777 double aisle aircraft with an option at 20 more.
Boeing also sells six Boeing Dreamliner jets to Oman Air, an operation valued at more than $1 billion.
Rare is news when a company gets an order of 50 units of its product.
But it's a big transaction when the company's aircraft maker Boeing Co. and the sale price for the 50 planes amounts to a record $18-billion.
It's as large as the place where Boeing received the order: Middle East, a growing gold mine of future aircraft orders.
In a study unveiled on Monday, the Chicago company estimates that over the next 20 years airlines in the Middle East will need 2,520 aircraft worth $450 billion.
Boeing announced over the weekend its longest-serving order for commercial jets worth dollars - 50 Boing 777 Double aisle jets.
The stuccess came from the Emirates airline in Dubai.
The airline also has an option to buy 20 more planes, which would raise the total sale price to $26 billion, Boeing announced in Dubai's 2011.
Later on Monday, Boeing said Oman Air had ordered six Boeing 787-8s, the so-called Dreamliner, which boasts fuel savings and spectacular passenger services.
The selling price for six of them is more than $1 billion.
"This clearly has an enormous positive effect for Boeing," Morningstar analyst Neal Dihora said.
"The quality of these particular customers is very strong."
Lots of Middle Eastern airlines are owned by their oil-producing nations.
Therefore, not only do they have the money to pay for planes, but an increase in fuel prices - usually harmful to airlines, because it is one of the highest costs - is not so harmful, because the country is making money from rising oil prices.
That's important because they're less likely than other airlines to cancel or delay flight orders when fuel prices increase, Dihora said.
"They have natural protection," she said.
U.S. order solidifies Boeing's lead in the wide fuselage aircraft market and helps keep its competi competitor Airbus SAS at bay's attempt to invade its dominance with the A350 passenger jet.
Boeing's victory at the air show comes four months after Airbus said it would delay the debut of the largest A350, which competes directly with the 777-300ER.
It delayed the debut to add more momentum after customers demanded an increase in the payload and range.
Boeing Co's shares rose 1.5 per cent to $67.94.
The sister of Mexico's president, apparently defeated in Michoacán's vote
Luisa María Calderón affirms that the drug traffickers helped their PRI opponent, Fausto Vallejo, in the contest for governor.
President Felipe Calderón's sister appears to have lost his candidacy for governor of Michoacán during the violent state elections, and she said on Monday that drug traffickers helped tilt the race for one of their opponents.
The preliminary results gave Fausto Vallejo, of the Institutional Revolutionary Revolutionary Party or the PRI in the race for governor of the western state.
"The intervention of organized crime during the entire electoral process and, above all yesterday, it is alarming, not only for Michoacán, but for the whole country," Luisa María Calderón said in a radio interview a day after Sunday's vote.
"They threatened our nominees, our electoral workers... they seized the polls, installed barricades... and ordered the people to vote" in favor of the PRI.
The PRI ruled Mexico for seven decades until it lost its presidency in 2000.
However, it's the stitch of a comeback, and the victory in Michoacán is an important step in that effort.
The PRI is hopeful of winning the presidential elections in July.
Vallejo seems to have narrowly surpassed Calderón, who had led the polls before the elections.
Calderón, a nominee of his brother's conservative National Action Party, or PAN, refused to recognize Vallejo as the winner.
Silvano Aureoles, the left-leaning nominee for the Democratic Revolution Party, or PRD, who currently holds the position of governor, came in third, a major defeat for the divided and unfortunate left before the 2012 presidential elections.
Aureoles also refused to acknowledge the preliminary results.
Vallejo, a former mayor of the state capital of Morelia, denied links to drug trafficking and urged the other candidates to accept the results.
Michoacán has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in marijuana, heroin and methamphetamine.
It's President Calderon's home state, and he chose Michoacán to launch a military offensive aimed at smugglers in December 2006.
However, the violence has persisted.
A PAN mayor was assassinated a week before the elections while campaigning for Luisa María Calderón, and numerous candidates abandoned local candidacies out of fear.
Although for the PRI, as unpleasant as the Michoacán elections may seem, a victory will help boost the party.
The man who is supposed to represent the PRI in the presidential elections, Enrique Peña Nieto, speaking from Washington, congratulated Vallejo and said: "I think this victory should be very encouraging for next year."
ADB Urges Asia to Help Resitate the Eurozone
The Asian Development Bank (BAsD) has called on India and China to prepare to help rescue the eurozone from its sovereign debt crisis to avoid a long-term recession that would prevent the growth of the Asian economies.
Rajat Nag, managing director of the Manila-based Asian Development Bank, said the world's two fastest-growing economies had to "make every effort" to accelerate the recovery of the economic bloc either through the International Monetary Fund or directly through bilateral agreements.
He cautioned against the BRIC [Brazil, Russia, India, China] countries observing Europe's difficulties in an "impartial" manner and said Asia's financial assistance along with European leadership and resources would help prevent a long-term crisis in the global economy.
"We're all in this together".
"So anyone who can help Europe get out of the crisis is helpful," he told the Financial Times in an interview as part of the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
"Asia can be protected to a certain extent, but it can't be Immune."
"So if China and India can help, let it be by all means."
Europe's monetary union has been badly undermined by a sovereign debt crisis in recent months.
Italy &apos; s borrowing costs have skyrocketed dangerously in recent days and the Prime Ministers of Italy and Greece have resigned.
Europe &apos; s current sovereign debt crisis has raised fears that it could push the global economy into recession and prompted calls for major emerging economies to come together to help the eurozone to find a solution.
Mr. Nag predicted that any support from Asia would be funnelled through the IMF, but he said bilateral assistance - such as the purchase of bonds from the EU bailout fund, the European Financial Stability Fund - offered greater bargaining power for Europe's Asian partners.
Anand Sharma, an Indian trade minister, said "India is going to do its utmost" to help the eurozone as its own economy was now suffering from falling exports and the depletion of foreign capital inflows.
"No one wants the eurozone to remain unstable and turbulent," he said.
"We have monumental challenges and we need to maintain a high level of growth."
"It's not an option, it's an imperative, 'because where do we find jobs for tens of millions of our young men and women."
Others argue that developing economies such as India have no responsibility to help wealthy Europeans when faced with their own deep economic problems.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University in the U.S., said it would be politically very difficult to sell aid to Europe for India's $1 billion, of whom up to $US800 million live on about $US2 a day or less.
"At some point they'll realise the Greeks are retiring at the age of 50 and they're going on vacation to the beaches and that won't go well," he said.
Lee Howell, Managing Director of the World Economic Forum, also wondered why India &apos; s bookings should be used to keep the many well-reliable Greek public sector workers in jobs of under-managed companies, and loss generators such as the railways.
Mr. Nag said the eurozone crisis threatens "important chain effects" across Asia.
The Asian Development Bank &apos; s forecast of 7.5 per per cent economic growth in Asia for 2011/12 is now facing "low-threp risks" due to Europe's threat.
He said vulnerable emerging markets needed "contingency plans" to protect themselves from a crisis and significant capital outflow from their economies.
Serious Pentagon warnings about possible defense cuts
U.S. Secretary of Defense León Panetta warmed Congress on Monday, warning that the looming automatic budget cuts could undermine national security and provoke a chain financial backlash from the Pentagon's aisles to the battlefields of Afghanistan to civilian assembly channels.
The Pentagon is already digesting the $450-billion-dollar reductions over the next decade, but it now fears an additional $600-billion-billion reduction in cuts that may be imminent if Congress fails to reach an agreement on spending.
"The impact of these cuts would be devastating for the department," Panetta said in a letter to senators John McCain, a Republican for Arizona and Lindsey Graham, Republican for South Carolina.
He said Congress's failure to reach an agreement on the budget and its consequence, the so-called "kidnapping," would result in 23 per cent overall reductions and a halt to many new projects.
"Such a large cut, implemented in this indiscriminate way, would ensure that most of our shipbuilding and construction projects cannot be implemented - you can't buy three-quarters of a ship or a building - and seriously damaging other modernization efforts," Panetta wrote to senators.
"We would also be obligated to remove many of our civilian officials involuntarily and, because the reduction would be imposed so quickly, it is almost certain that we would have to discharge civilians in order to achieve the objective."
"These changes would tear the faith with those who maintain our army and seriously damage their willingness."
Crusades would eventually reach combat troops, Panetta said.
"Whether wartime funding in continge operations accounts overseas is not directly affected by kidnapping, war efforts would be adversely affected by the serious upheaval in grassroots budgets," Panetta warned.
"The recruitment of staff would be reduced, which would lead to delays in contracts and the supervision of the contract that supports the war."
"Ployment staff would shrink, leading to delays in payments to wartime suppliers, and legal and political support would be disrupted."
The two senators had written to Panetta 10 days ago, asking for information about the possible impact of automatic cuts at the Department of Defense.
"The consequence of a kidnapping at the Department of Defense would lead to a rapid decline of the United States as the world's leading military power."
"We are firmly against this draconian action," the senators said in a joint statement Monday afternoon when they published Panetta's letter.
'It's not a result you can't live with, and it's certainlyn't one we should impose on ourselves.'
"Kidnapping is a threat to America's national security interests, and it should not be allowed to happen."
Whether Panetta's letter and senators' recent warnings will increase pressure for a budget compromise or intensify calls to exempt the Pentagon from the cuts remains to be seen.
Panetta has been increasingly candid about the possible cuts, even though he came to a job at the top of the Pentagon with years of experience in budgets in Congress and the White House and knowing he was facing tough decisions.
At a press conference last week, the Defense Secretary painted a bleak picture of what might lie ahead - an army with a shell, but not a core.
"It's a ship without sailors".
"It's a no-blooding brigade".
"It's an aerial wing without enough trained pilots".
"It's a paper tiger, an army of barracks, buildings and bombs without enough soldiers trained to fulfill the mission," Panetta said in his opening remarks at the Pentagon.
"It's a force that suffers from low morality, poor preparation and is unable to keep up with potential adversaries."
"Indeed, it invites aggression...."
In an appendage to his letters to McCain and Graham, Panetta sets out new details of how the reductions "generate significant operational risks: delays response time to crises, conflicts and disasters; severely limits our ability to be previously deployed and compromised around the world; and poses an unacceptable risk in future combat operations."
And Panetta said some of the biggest defense projects could face the axe, including those that are already being tested and a few only in the early stages of planning.
That list includes the F35 Joint Strike Fighter fighter jet, a planned new bomber, the next-generation ballistic submarine, the new coastal combat ship and the new ground-to-sea combat vehicle that the Army and the Navy need to rebuilt the humvee.
Stopping the F35's development and further testing could generate about $80 billion in savings over 10 years, but its supporters say it's the next vital step in improving and addressing potential threats from China and other rivals.
How to detect a lying
A glimpse of the most recent headlines tells us how fierce and ubiquitous deceit and lying in daily life.
Republican presidential nominee Herman Cain is busy exchanging allegations of sexual harassment with several women; each side accuses of lying to each other.
Administrators at Pennsylvania State University have been charged with perjury for allegedly concealing reports that a retired soccer coach was sexually assaulting children.
Last week, the French president, Nicholas Sarkozy, was "picked" with an outspoken microphone claiming U.S. President Barack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is a liar.
Lying has destroyed racing and convulsed countries.
The congresswoman for New York and exhibitionist on the Internet, Anthony Weiner, made a fool of his denials quickly rebuffed by irrefutable proof.
Former presidential nominee John Edwards has been charged with campaign finance violations related to the cover-up of an extramarital relationship.
And, again, no one who lived it will ever forget the media circus unleashed by President Bill Clinton by lying during his second term about his sexual relationship with Monica Lewinsky.
Legends on cheating in school and university exams are widespread.
There have been cases in which teachers have given exam responses to students in order to be better seen in their performance evaluations.
Mentors who should be teaching otherwise are sending the message that lying and deceiving are acceptable.
How deceit do we find?
On any-on-day, studies show they can lying to you anywhere from 10 to 200 times.
Sure, many of those lies are pious lyres.
Another study showed that strangers lied three times in the first 10 minutes of getting to know each other.
To detect lies, or "locing lying," is an essential skill that we must all acquire, for both personal and professional reasons.
Far from being a board game similar to, for example, Charada, where the goal is to exclaim: "I've got you," deceit detection is a branch of serious knowledge based on scientific data obtained over the last six decades at prestigious universities that carried out in-depth research projects, especially in psychology and physiology.
One of the results of the investigation is that old myths about the lying have refuted.
The liars do look in the eye.
They don't always stutter, babble, blush or uneasy.
It cannot also be concluded that liars are difficult to detect or unmask.
A trained lying detector can come to the truth by learning about the structure of the statement, the microfacial expressions, the formation of questions and the times.
I spent several years probing scientific discoveries about deceiving in the vast and ever-emerging body of knowledge, and it was clear that detecting deception is a modern technician that's easy to Learn and helps navigate our complex world - especially if his professional responsibilities include hiring, interviews, or negotiating management.
Good liars have the ability to read others well, making them feel at ease, managing their own emotions and intuitively settling how others perceive them.
We know from research that extroverts lying more than introverts that men say more "self-facing lies," while women say more "thought-for-thready" lies -- usually to protect someone's feelings -- that married people are less often lying to their partners than unmarried people (but the lies they usually say they tend to be "huge").
We also know that if you're perceived as an evildoer, others will feel less culprits in lying to you.
How do you know if someone's lying?
First of all, look at the guy's usual behavior.
It's called "creating a baseline."
It helps to provide benchmarks to then measure the changes.
Observe the subject's posture, laughter and vocal feature.
You'd better know if someone normally gets punching with your foot all the time so you don't make unfair accusations when you see your feet punching in the middle of the meeting.
Then look for deceptive, verbal and non-verbal behavioral groups.
Consider these behavioral groups as alarm signals, not proof of deceivation.
Deceiving people can freeze the upper part of the body by trying to remember their history, can point their feet to the door, lean towards an exit, change their posture significantly or display a "post-interview release" - that exaggerated sigh of relief and change of posture once all difficult questions are over.
interrogators often falsely show that the interview is over only to seek that post-intermediated relief.
It also pays attention to the subject's vocabulary.
Scott Peterson screwed himself to the bottom and used the time spent as he claimed his murdered wife was alive, launching a nationwide quest.
Deceiving people can also use remote language: "I didn't have sex with that woman ... Miss Lewinsky" or repeating a difficult question entirely.
The most common verbal indicators are subtle.
Someone can use a bunch of "restrictive language" when it answers a difficult question: "Well... to tell you the truth... as far as I know.."
This makes the response a perception rather than a fact and is often a warning signal.
There's no magical formula for detecting lying, but developing skills to discover deception is possible.
These skills will improve anyone's ability to avoid being victimized by scam artists in their professional and personal lives.
Controversial sprawn pipeline plan diverted after U.S. lag after U.S. delay in approval
Days after the Obama administration threatened to delay the approval of a planned pipeline from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico -- angering the unions while appeasing environmentalists -- the company seeking to build the pipeline says it's willing to reroute the project to re-route it.
TransCanada said on Monday night it will move the planned pipeline out of the sensitive Sandhills area in Nebraska, and is confident the project will win approval.
The company announced the decision at a press conference at the Nebraska Capitol.
TransCanada CEO Alex Pourbax says the company remains confident the pipeline will eventually be approved, although it's true that with a different route.
This comes after the State Department's announcement last this week that they would delay the decision for a federal permit for the project until new potential routes are studied to avoid the Sandhills areas of Nebraska and the Ogallala aquifer, a vast supply of groundwater.
"That's a real avenue of progress," Rep. Lee Terry, a Republican from Nebraska, told Fox News.
The new environmental review ordered by the State Department may set aside any decisions until 2013, after next year's election, though officials denied that the policy was wrapped up in the decision.
Mr. Obama, in a written statement last week, described the decision as the State Department's request, and expressed his full support.
The 1,700-kilometre-kilometre pipeline would stretch from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico.
It would bring 700,000 barrels a day from the province of Alberta to refineries in Texas.
To do so, it would be necessary for him to cross six states.
It's hated by ecologists but loved by the working groups, who have bet on the estimate of 20,000 jobs linked to the pipeline.
But Nebraska lawmakers opposing the plan are weighing the legislation to force a move away from the Sandhills region and the Ogallala aquifer, the largest source of drinking water and irrigation.
The State Department &apos; s current environmental impact study found that the project only had limited adverse environmental impacts, but the energy industry source said the department's inspector general has ordered an independent test of the review process, focusing on two issues.
It's one whether TransCanada-hired lobby member Paul Elliot, who was Hillary Clinton's campaign adviser in 2008, represents a conflict of interest for the program.
The other is whether a firm that was hired to run the original studio was an inappropriate choice because it was linked to TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle sued for allegedly provoking a child to vomit with scary
A New NYC family claims that disco music diva, Patti LaBelle, behaved like crazies in the lobby of her Manhattan building, terrifying her young daughter to the point that she vomited with fear.
The "Lady Marmalade" singer diatribe scared 18-month-old Genevieve Monk that she suffered "personality changes, sleep disorders" and "increased her scary from strangers," says her family in the Manhattan Supreme Court.
It's November 10 last year when stretcher Kevin Monk, his wife, Roseanna, a kindergarten teacher, and Genevieve were preparing to go on a family trip.
As Kevin Monk picked up the car, Roseanna Monk descended into the lobby of the Riverside Boulevard building with her luggage carrying Genevieve.
Roseanna Monk said she stood near the gate and put her daughter on the ground for a moment.
"Someone came from behind and said, "Do you know what your daughter's doing?" I said 'Yeah' and I went to catch her," she said.
LaBelle admonished angry the pregnant woman.
"She said in an aggressive tone, "Shouldn't have left your daughter near the door,"" Roseanna Monk said.
"I said, "I don't have any interest in whatever you say or think."
He became anguried and began to use bad words - the C-and-word-word J out loud...
He had a bottle of water and he started throwing the water from the bottle over me."
She said Genevieve got hysterical.
"I said to this woman, "Look what you've done to my daughter," the girl's mum said.
LaBelle pounced on the mother -- who was still holding Genevieve -- and had to be restrained because of her surroundings and removed from a car waiting for her, Roseanna Monk said.
The little girl was crying so loud she vomited, she added.
Later, the family sent a note asking for an apology from LaBelle, but did not get an answer.
LaBelle's lawyer and representatives did not return the calls to comment on what had happened.
The lawsuit seeks unspecified damages, but the family's lawyer, Sam Davis, said he took the case for free and that the family plans to donate all the money he gets to a support association for children with cancer.
"It's the purpose to make Patti LaBelle responsible for his conduct," Davis said.
"That kind of behavior is completely unacceptable, especially when you turn to a kindergarten teacher who carries an 18-month-old child in her arms."
Climate Adversity Stopped the Economy
Mario Lopez Valdez's first year of management has increased in Sinaloa unemployment and informality, foreign investment fell and the economy contracted
2011 is a year that will be remembered in the economic history of Sinaloa, it is the first year of government of Mario López Valdez, self-denomination of change, which generated great expectations regarding the advances in the economy, but given the climatic adversity at the beginning of the year, there is no growth, on the other there is a deterioration in the main indicators of economic development.
The low temperatures of February, in addition to causing million-dollar losses for the agricultural sector, limited the possibilities of the state economy to grow, caused a contraction of overall economic activity of 3.6 percent in the first half of the year, justified mainly by the historic fall of 31.16 per cent in agriculture, which affected the dynamism of the other economic sectors.
According to the Frozen Frozen Affectation Report, issued by the Mexican Council for Sustentable Rural Development, there was damage to 582,835 hectares cultivated, of which 78.03 per cent suffered total damage and the remaining 21.07 per cent had partial damage, the main unleashed crops of vegetables, corn and lizard.
The fall in economic activity has generated, until November, a constant increase in unemployment in the state, according to the National Institute of Statistics and Geography the state unemployment rate during the first three quarters of the year accelerated growth, since from January to March 55,053 Sinaloans were unoccupied, with a rate of 4.53 per cent of the economically active population, for the second quarter the rate increased to 5.28 per cent, and between July and September the average continued to increase to 6.19 per cent, a rate that in the number of people yielded to more than 74 thousand Unemployed Sinaloans, an increase from the first semester of 18,969 individuals.
Reduced liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa during the year is multi-factor, for the economist Gerardo López Cervantes, director of the Faculty of Economic and Social Sciences of the UAS, the growth of unemployment can be explained indirectly, depending on the actions that have been carried out from public policy.
"What you're seeing is an increase in unemployment that has to do with a number of factors, including the loss of liquidity in the market due to the effects of a restriction of resources by the legislation that was approved in Congress to combat money laundering, already for people who grab dollars illegally it's not easy for them to work, invest them and use them in something," he said.
Detained migration
Lopez Cervantes pointed out that the increase in unemployment there is also a demographic factor.
"The number of people leaving the state in search of work abroad, in the U.S. it has been curtailed, first because of the difficulties in getting in, and second because they already have a lot of difficulty finding a job, these people are staying and are demanding sources of work here in the state, which causes unemployment to rise," he said.
Informality
The analysis of the Sinaloan labour market is aggravated by considering the high level of informality that predominates between working conditions, according to the INEGI, 60.38 per cent of the employed population in the state works without quoting as a worker before any social security institution.
In addition to unemployment and informality, another of the lags in the state are low wages, the entity has the lowest salaries in the country, according to the records of the Mexican Institute of Social Security and the Ministry of Labour and Social Security.
The median monthly income for Sinaloan workers was 5,000 352 pesos, well below the national average of 7, 375 pesos.
Relocation of FDI
In addition to the fall in economic activity and the increase in unemployment, during 2011 there has been the largest decrease in direct foreign investment of the last 10 years, according to the Ministry of Economy from January to June of state FDI totaled $630,697, representing a fall of 87.74 per cent compared to 5 million 143,312 dollars recorded during the same period of the previous year.
For its part, the Ministry of Economic Development of the State Government counts a total investment of 17, 280 million pesos in the year, which occurred mainly from companies in the tertiary sector.
Definition
Faced with the scenario of economic contraction, the state government asked the state congress in July for the approval of a credit for 3 billion pesos, which would be allocated the investment in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities, in order to reactivate the economy, the approval of the local congress was in the amount of 2.6 billion pesos, and until November they have not observed the benefits of indebtedness in any sector of the state economy.
Reaction
According to representatives of the business sector, Mario López Valdés &apos; first year's government shows a lack of government management capacity.
Miguel Loredo Lopez, president of the National Chamber of the Transformation Industry, said that during 2011 he lacked the harmonization of the wills of the different sectors, including the state government itself.
"The performance of the economy in Sinaloa during this year has been really very poor, we have to work intensely to recover the terrain that we lost this year, in 2012 we have to redouble our efforts, there has been a lack to align everyone's efforts and above all the well-clenchronized progress of this new government team, we hope that next year will be much better," he said.
Exclusive economic focus
Loredo Lopez stated that events such as frosts at the beginning of the year stopped the state's economy and restricted its performance and growth.
"The low economic flow is the product of a number of events, including frosts, one of the main effects has been a sweeping economy that doesn't turn around fast enough to generate an economic dynamism that allows to have an acceptable level of flow in the processes, products and services and therefore, at the end of the year we are closing well below the expectations we had," he said.
Redefinite vocation
For Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, president of the Patternal Confederation of the Mexican Republic in Sinaloa, he said it is necessary to prosecute the state towards productive activities of greater dynamism.
"In general terms we have noticed that work has been done, we believe it is necessary to develop a long-term program that redefines the vocation and economic direction of the state, because it is important to detonate the entire material and human resource that Sinaloa has," he said.
I'm working to do.
The president of Coparmex stressed the importance of establishing a vision for the future that better channels the government's efforts.
"In general terms I would say that the government has been striving to achieve good results in various areas, in terms of economic development there is some progress but it is important that a long-term programme be developed," he said.
He pointed out that the current administration has shown progress in the way public policy is conducted.
"There are elements that make it clear that the government is becoming more inclusive, what I can say is that there is work done and I think the government is aware that there is work to be done," he said.
He is convicted of 8kg of marijuana
After five years in prison and a 100-day fine, a neighbour of Navolato was sentenced for the possession of eight kilos of marijuana.
The Attorney General &apos; s Office of the Attorney-General &apos; s Office reported that the convicted narcomenudist is Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval for the crime against health, in his variant of possession of marijuana for commercial purposes.
