He mentioned that last year, in the administration of Jesús Aguilar Padilla, the State did not comply with the corresponding contributions, so at the beginning of the administration the Municipality had to request a credit to cover the more than 7 million that the state administration did not contribute on that occasion.
"It has already happened to us in the previous six years because the state did not have the economic capacity to participate and the programs kept walking, and this year we to be able to access these programs we had to obtain the resources to cover what the state left pending," he said.
Although there is the commitment of the state authorities to comply with the delivery of these resources, he commented that the request has been made to the Secretary of Administration and Finance, Armando Villarreal, to hasten the efforts so that the percentages that correspond to them arrive as soon as possible.
"It's almost 12 million they have to contribute and even though the year is about to end, they haven't given us a fifth."
Cut valid "Analog blackout" in Mexico in 2015
Replacing analog signals with digital is one of the most important changes that the world has undergone in recent years.
The Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) dismissed the constitutional controversies filed by the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies against the presidential decree advancing the so-called "analog blackout" from 2021 to 2015, by not gathering the eight votes necessary to invalidate it.
A minority of four ministers, including the President of the Court, Juan Silva Meza, considered that President Felipe Calderón did not exceed his powers by issuing the decree detailing the actions to be taken to realize the transition to digital terrestrial television.
With this result, the constitutional dispute promoted by the Senate of the Republic was dismissed for failing to reach an eight-vote qualified majority for the Court to rule on the unconstitutionality or unconstitutionality of the decree, and it remains in force.
The draft judgment, prepared by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero, proposed declaring the decree unconstitutional, because the regulation of radio and television matters is a power reserved by law to the Federal Telecommunications Commission (Cofetel), in violation of the principles of hierarchical subordination and the reservation of law.
In favor of the project they voted only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero herself.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies promoted two constitutional controversies to challenge the decree, and by admitting the lawsuits, Minister Sánchez Cordero suspended the application of the decree and all the actions provided for the digital transition.
The plenary of ministers also decided to dismiss the lawsuit filed by the Chamber of Deputies, without going into the discussion of the project, also by Minister Sánchez Cordero.
In his speech, the Minister President said he did not agree with the arguments of the project that the federal executive should not be able to structuring through general rules the measures for the public administration to prepare.
"I also don't believe that if it did, the autonomy of the regulatory body would be affected," and he recalled that the planning of public policies in strategic areas such as the telecommunications sector corresponds to the federal executive, which has a constitutional mandate, the rectory of the state.
Replacing analog-to-digital signals is one of the most important changes the world has undergone in recent years, resulting in a better use of the radio spectrum, better signal quality and more channels.
To postpone the change, he said, would be to postpone the benefits of digitization, which implies a decision of the public interest, which does not only have a technical dimension, "because it covers economic, social, political and national security issues."
These issues, he stressed, "require the operation of the entire established system, in accordance with the principle of economic leadership of the State, includes a group of powers that the Constitution leads to the power of the State Executive."
He recalled that a large number of countries around the world have already completed the digital transition, and has done so through the Executive, despite highly specialized technical bodies.
It cannot be concluded that the Executive, in issuing the contested decree, which in some way invades the sphere of jurisdiction of the Congress, since the observance of the laws issued by the legislature is being provided in the administrative sphere, he said.
Analyze the possibility of a "vote-by-vote" count in Michoacán
According to the data released by the PREP the difference between first and second place of the governor's election is just 27 per cent, even without counting the votes corresponding to 879 of the 6,000 74 total minutes.
The possibility of "vote-by-voting" count is a reality with regard to this year's electoral process in Michoacán, where, according to the data released by the Preliminary Electoral Results Program (PREP), the difference between the first and second place of the governor's election is only 27 percent, even without counting the votes corresponding to 879 of the 6,000 74 total minutes.
In this regard, the President of the General Council of the Electoral Institute of Michoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that a few days a few days ago, the electoral body approved a series of guidelines in order to harmonize local norms with the federal electoral reform.
It will be recalled that Michoacán did not adopt his Political Constitution or State Electoral Code or its Electoral Justice Act, since the State Congress approved the reforms shortly before the end of the legal period so that they could enter into force in the present electoral process and Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel skimmed its publication within the time limits provided for in the Law on the Official Journal of the State, on the grounds that he had some objections.
On November 9, the Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) resolved the constitutional dispute promoted by the local legislature and described as "invalid and illegal" the state president &apos; s vote for constitutional reforms, since he does not have the right to "sanctify or veto them."
Interviewed about the possibility of the "vote by vote," María de los Ángeles Llanderal stated that thanks to the approval of the corresponding guidelines by the General Council of the IEM it will be possible to carry out partial and total recounts of the vote by the local electoral body.
In this way, the partial counting of votes, of one or more electoral packages, may take place in the event that: the results of the minutes do not coincide; there is no record of scrutiny and computation in the record of the box or does not work in the possession of the box; or, when there are obvious errors or alterations of the minutes.
In addition, the full recount of the vote received in all boxes of the election may take place when there is an indication that the difference between the presumptive candidate the winner of the election in question and whoever has obtained the second place in the vote is equal to or less by one percentage point; provided that at the beginning of the session there is the express request of the representative of the party and/or coalition who nominated the second of the above-mentioned candidates.
The total counting of the vote may also be carried out if at the end of the ordinary computation it is established that the difference between the allegedly winning candidate and the one placed second is equal to or less by a percentage point, as long as there is the express request of the representative of the political party whose candidate remained in the second place, in which the electoral packages that have been partially recounted will be excluded.
It should be mentioned that in this situation there is the election of Morelia, where according to the PREP the difference between the first and the second place is only 0.16 per cent, although without counting the votes of 129 out of the 923 total minutes.
