The new Italian government will be formed on Wednesday
The appointed Prime Minister, Mario Monti, will tomorrow meet with the president of Italy to present the new government that will face a crisis that has placed that country on the verge of the economic disaster
Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet Italy's president on Wednesday to present the new government that will face a crisis that has placed Italy on the verge of economic disaster and put the entire eurozone at risk.
A statement from the presidential palace announced that Monti, appointed on Sunday, will meet Wednesday with the president, Giorgio Napolitano, to confirm that he can form a government.
He is expected to present a cabinet composed mostly of technocrats, although it is not clear when the new executive will take office.
Monti said he will present to the president the outcome of his talks with the political parties in his attempt to form a government.
"I want to confirm my absolute confidence in our country's ability to overcome this difficult phase," the former European commissioner told reporters.
Monti did not explicitly say that he will be able to form a government but the tone of his comments indicated the hurdles have been overcome.
"Marco is now well delineated," he added.
Monti has completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, much less than normal, while Italy is fighting a political and financial crisis that has driven the costs of its debt to unsustainable levels.
The new administration led by former European commissioner Monti must approve a tough austerity package demanded by European leaders to regain political confidence in Italy.
Underlining the pressure on Monti to rush, market turmoil put the yield on Italian 10-year BTP bonds above 7 per cent, the level at which Greece and Ireland were forced to be rescued.
Emma Marcegaglia, leader of the Italian bosses Confindustria, told the press after meeting with Monti: "We said that we would support this government very much."
We believe that this Government is the last opportunity for Italy to emerge from this emergency situation.
Monti &apos; s chances of success were significantly boosted by Silvio Berlusconi's PDL party's PDL, to which the crisis forced to resign on Saturday.
"We believe that Professor Monti's efforts are destined to have a good result," Angelino Alfano, secretary of the centre-right party, told the press.
PDL support, Italy &apos; s longest-largest party, is significant because so far many of its members had opposed the technocrat-majority-majority government Monti.
Parliamentary support
Monti &apos; s new government must have strong parliamentary support to implement what is likely to be unpopular austerity reforms.
Any blocking or delay in their efforts could lead to a new and devastating attack from the financial markets.
Italy's association of foreign banks added to the pressure by warning that a failure of Monti would be a disaster.
Monti on Monday began his consultations with political parties, trade unions and business groups, as well as with youth and women's organizations.
He'll put an end to his round of meetings on Tuesday night.
Monti was appointed on Sunday by Napolitano, which has enabled an extremely rapid transition in response to the crisis.
After a brief respite at the end of last week, when it became clear that Berlusconi would resign, Italian debt costs have now returned to critical levels amid uncertainty about whether the new prime minister will succeed.
Rescuing Italy, with a public debt of 1.8 trillion euros, would be too too much for the eurozone's current financial defenses.
Monti has said that his government should last until the next elections, scheduled for 2013, despite widespread predictions that politicians will only give them time to implement the reforms before advancing the elections.
Monti has said that he would like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the big parties insist it must be made up only of technicians, a hint of their qualifiers about a process forced by financial pressure.
Political sources pointed out that mutual mistrust and party disagreements are complicating the attempt to include political figures.
