Quinteto included the country in its two-decade celebratory to tour
"Going to Costa Rica is a dream come true".
Exclusive in an exclusive interview with Viva, bassist Jeff Ament of Pearl Jam said that the band is ready to offer one of their best concerts next Sunday, at the National Stadium.
There have been more than 20 years of waiting, but the band assures that for them it will also be to make reality the dream of playing in the country a reality.
This was assured by the bass player and founding member, Jeff Ament, who spoke exclusively with Viva, from Brazil, last week.
The group is located in the South American wing of its two-degree celebration tour and on Sunday the 20th it will make its only stopover in Central America.
On that day they will be presented at the National Stadium, starting at 7:30 p.m., next to the Angel band The X and the nationals of Las Robertas.
This is an exaggeration of the conversation, whose full transcript is on The Nation on the Internet.
For its Costa Rican followers, it's a dream come true that Pearl Jam is going to play in Costa Rica, are they aware of what their fans are living?
Going to Costa Rica is a dream come true for us too.
It's barely our second time in South and Central America and so far it's been great.
I've been in Costa Rica only once and on vacation; it's such a beautiful country and I think we're gonna be able to stay there for a few days.
So we're excited to bring our music there.
I'm wondering because there's a sort of devotion to the band, the fans live intensely the days before.
What do they say to their most fervent followers?
Thank you for staying at our side throughout all these years.
It took us almost 21 years to get to Costa Rica; thank you for having waited so long and we're sorry that it's taken us so long to get there.
Some of the people even refer to his gig as the biggest show of the year.
Does that add a bit of pressure to your visit?
Some of the biggest shows we've played over the past 21 years happened over the last couple of weeks.
We've played a couple of gigantic shows in Sao Paulo and we're hoping that'll put us in shape for this concert in Costa Rica.
I'm really eager to play there.
We love that country, we love surfing and the jungle; for both, Costa Rica is among the best in the world.
How did they discover Costa Rica and its surfing waves?
We've got plenty of friends who are surfers and I've got a great friend who lives in Tamarindo and the Waves there are incredible.
My friends would always surf Costa Rica and Nicaragua, along with Mexico, they are great places to surf; that's how we found out, through people who love surf.
Looking at the to to tour, they've been delivering huge shows with lots of songs.
What have they planned for presentation in Costa Rica?
Since we've never played there before, then we don't have any restrictions on what we can do.
We'll be trying to give the best possible show with a good variety of songs; we'll try to play a couple of songs from each album and give our fans a nice and diverse repertoire.
You'll never repertoire repeat the repertoire.
- What's the reason for this?
I think it's partly because we have nine albums and we can play more than 120 songs and everyone likes different songs, so it's hard to choose which songs to play every night.
When we have two or three songs in the repertoire that we've beenn't playing for a while, that keeps us focused and it also keeps us surprised because it reminds us of the reasons why we liked those songs and could have been forgotten.
That makes the show more interesting for us and, therefore, I think it also makes it more interesting for people.
With this to tour they are celebrating 20 years of trajectory.
Looking back, is it hard to believe everything they've achieved?
- Yeah, absolutely.
First of all, I don't think any of us imagine we'd be on a big rock band and have done it for more than 20 years makes it look like a dream.
We are still telling our blessings and we are very excited that we can keep doing music together.
We all love each other and we love to create music.
We're lucky to go to places in the world we've always wanted to go.
Things couldn't be better for us.
What'd it meant to you to have been a fundamental part of the rise of the grunge?
Always it's been an honor to be part of that group of big bands.
We're very friends with the people of Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice ins Chain.
Surprising when a movement emerges from a bunch of friends and it's uncommon.
Occasionally, a band emerges from a city, but it's much stranger when five or ten bands come out at the same time from the same city.
We are proud and honored to be part of the Seattle group.
We're still bumping with some of them every now and then.
Over the past year, we were at a festival with Alice in Chains and we were in Canada with Mudhoney, we saw Soundgarden on his tour, so we're lucky to be friends with all of them yet and we're honored to be part of that Seattle sound that emerged.
It's also very weird that a band like yours is sticking together and with the same members for so long.
Yeah, four of us have been together for 21 years and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago, which, by itself, is much longer than many bands last.
First of all, I think we're all lucky to still be alive and, secondly, we all care about each other.
We are all very close friends and, thanks to that, there have also been musical avenues that give freedom to everyone to experience.
We confiding each other with each other's music and it couldn't be better.
The relationship of you with the word fame has not been simple; however, thanks to its success, you have been able to do whatever you want.
How do you find a balance?
As we've been growing, it's been eager to find a balance.
We make sure we don't be too toured because it's easy to fall into that game of the big crowds and the people who love your music.
As long as one remembers that people are there for the music, it reminds you that you have to respect the music and what you brought it, without infacing for being famous and being a rock star, something that none of us really care about.
How do you remember your other Mother Love Bone project?
I think we wrote very good songs and at the beginning it was a great thing.
We've been a band for almost three years and we never released an album until after Andy (Andrew Woods died).
There's been a lot of frustration because it lasted so long and the most important thing is that we're still very friends with Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore), who started in the band with us and when I hear a song or I see a photo I think of the incredible person that Andy was and how he made me laugh, it was always a pleasure to go to work, laugh, talk about music and soccer and a lot more.
He was an astonishing human being and we miss him.
The national band Las Robertas will be in charge of opening the show in Costa Rica.
Is it true that you guys know your work, especially Eddie Vedder, and that's why you've been picked for openness?
Yeah, the production sent us videos with several proposals and that was the band we liked the most, so we're eager to see what Las Robertas can offer live and must be very entertaining.
Why do they always change Yellow Ledbetter's lyrics live?
That's a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), so I don't know.
He shoots it and I think he's doing the first thing he can think of.
As a bass player, what's his influences?
It's so many.
Among the first to mind are Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski...
There's so many that I could talk all afternoon about the bass players I love.
How did Pearl Jam manage to keep the grunge relevant even though it wasn't already a fashion anymore?
I think all of us always feel a bit disconnected from the grunge word.
When I think of it, it reminds me of the bands that I mentioned before, like Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana and all those that emerged at the same time at the same time, and, if there's relevance right now, I think it's a wonderful thing because it's music that's worth listening and bands like Mudhoney are now making better music than ever.
At the beginning, his music had darker content and has now become more positive.
Why is this change?
It's interesting because I think there's a couple of songs on the new album that may seem lighter, but there are also songs, such as The End or Just Breathe qualifiers as very dark songs although they may sound a little positive.
With both, when Ed touched them, I cried when I listened to them and they're deeply obscure songs.
"I think when you grow up and there's some trauma in your life and you lose a family member or a friend, those things begin to influence your art and your music and those two songs certainly represent that for me.
Also, I think you can be a Happy Person and still be able to make music that's obscure.
- Peru:
Fujimori is re-interned for medical examinations
Former President Alberto Fujimori was hospitalized Tuesday in a public hospital to be evaluated for the loss of muscle strength in his legs, his GP reported.
Fujimori, 72, who is serving a 25-year prison sentence for human rights violations committed during his government (1990-2000), has several ailments, including cancer in the language for which he has undergone surgery four times and remains under control.
Congressman Alejandro Aguinaga, who is his personal GP, told Canal N of television that Fujimori will be evaluated for three days at the National Institute of Neoplastic Diseases to determine the reason for the pain and the loss of strength of its lower members.
Aguinaga commented that Fujimori in his opinion would be a candidate to obtain the benefit of the pardon for humanitarian reasons, however, he said he does not want it because he knows himself innocent.
He was operated on four times of cancer to the tongue, has a weight loss of 18 kilos to which other pathologies such as severe erosive gastritis, the cyst of the pancreas, kidney stones, high blood pressure and circulatory problems in his or her legs have been combined, the doctor listed.
Fujimori has been in custody since September 2007 in the National Directorate of Special Operations of the police.
In April 2009, he was sentenced to 25 years &apos; imprisonment for the deaths of 25 people at the hands of annihilation of the army who operated clandestinely in the early years of his government.
Mujica travels to Mexico to strengthen political ties
An official delegation, headed by Uruguayan President José Mujica and composed of several ministers and businessmen, will begin a visit to Mexico on Tuesday in order to expand trade between the two nations and address political issues in the region.
Mujica will meet next Wednesday with his Mexican partner, Felipe Calderón, the highest point of an extensive agenda of meetings with political, commercial authorities and a meeting with the collectivity of Uruguayans residing in Mexico, according to the agenda disseminated by the Uruguayan Presidency.
The meeting between the two presidents, in the city of Guadalajara, will be "appropriate for the exchange of information in all political matters between the two nations," as published by the Presidency of the South American country on its website.
According to local media, Mujica would seek to get Calderón to support Uruguay in the incident with France that arose when the French president, Nicolas Sarkozy, included the South American country in a list of tax havens at the G20 meeting in the French city of Cannes.
In his speech, Sarkozy threatened to exclude from the international community those countries that would still be tax havens, a declaration that provoked the rejection of Uruguay and the call for consultation of its ambassador in the European country.
Since November, Mexico has presided over the G20, a group made up of the world &apos; s most powerful emerging and developed countries, and has in place with the South American country, since January this year, an agreement to exchange tax information in order to avoid double taxation.
The Uruguayan government has tried in recent years to expedite the implementation of tax-exchange agreements in order to emerge from the "grey list" of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), formed by countries that did not implement all international standards of fiscal cooperation.
At the commercial level, Mexico and Uruguay have in force since 2004 a Free Trade Agreement (FTA) that has increased the exchange of goods between the two nations.
The moustacholed plumber spends the three dimensions with "Super Mario 3D Land"
Super Mario has struggled, untiringly for 25 years to save Princess Peach from the claws of the wicked Bowser, but she has never done so in three dimensions until now: in "Super Mario 3D Land" she will fight the girl's love in stereoscopic tech.
The title, which arrives this month in stores from all over the world, has been designed from the outset to take advantage of Nintendo 3DS portable console, which allows you to play in three dimensions without the need to wear glasses.
"Mario is the character who has to say how stereoscopy should work without glasses," said Nintendo's specialized press manager in Spain, Omar Alvarez, during the presentation of the video game in Madrid.
Alvarez indicated that "Super Mario 3D Land" is " Nintendo's first video game originally designed for that support," since until now successes such as "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of Time 3D" were adaptations of existing titles.
Nintendo's spokesman has maintained that, despite being in 3D, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "accessible" video game that allows you to enjoy a quick and fluid game.
Alvarez has stated that "Super Mario 3D Land" contains two games in one: the first part is "simple" to hook less skilled users.
Once surpassed, the most experts will be able to take a "second turn" at the levels of the game, which this time present the greatest difficulty.
In addition, users will be able to choose in real time between an "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of the action, but it will be necessary to play in three dimensions in order to be able to overcome the different challenges and "do not succumb to optical illusions."
This incursion of Super Mario in the three dimensions also represents the return of the character to a portable console, a fact that did not take place since 2005.
In this video game Nintendo winks the followers of the saga and repetites the formula of the success of the previous installments, consisting that the moustache plumber will have to to tour endangered platforms with the aim of liberating Princess Peach.
Although the player will find novelties in the movements, the environments, the costumes and the enemies and also a "replayable" Luigi.
Colom endorses Portillo's extradition to the U.S.
President Álvaro Colom announced on Tuesday that he processed the extradition of the former governor Alfonso Portillo to answer in the United States for the conspiracy to launder $70 million.
This ends the process for Guatemala to respond to the North's request for Portillo to try him there.
At a press conference, Colom stated that his decision obeys the independence of the judiciary and the executive, taking into account that a sentencing court had already ordered extradition.
He stated that during his tenure he "didn't put his hands" in court decisions and processed all the extraditions he's known.
In addition to Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín will be extradited for murder and serious injuries, and Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes, both for drug trafficking.
What was done by Colom implies an executive procedure after all judicial and constitutional bodies were exhausted to prosecute the former president between 2000 and 2004.
In accordance with decisions of the Fifth Criminal Court and the Constitutional Court (CCC), Portillo must be extradited once his legal situation has been resolved in Guatemala and his human rights are guaranteed.
Portillo was absolved by the embezzlement of Q120 million in the Ministry of Defense when he was president, which was the process that preceded the extradition, but the Public Prosecutor's Office appealed and reactivated the case.
Trinijove has helped 6,000 young people at risk
The entity was born to avoid the fall in the marginalization of untrained children
The assessment of the first 25 years of life of the social support entity Trinijove is important: 6,000 young people at risk of exclusion have been helped to overcome a difficult situation throughout this quarter century.
Added to this is the hundred long-term jobs currently achieved for this social group.
Yesterday the anniversary served to take stock at an event celebrated at the former Fabra i Coats manufacturing complex attended by more than 300 people.
Trinijove was born in the Trinitat Vella neighborhood in 1986 with the aim of helping young people with little training, without work, with dogradation problems or in a position to fall into marginality.
Yesterday's celebration in Sant Andreu focused on a large number of leaders of entities and institutions that in these 25 years have collaborated with the association.
The varied political representation, in the middle of an electoral time, showed the unitary nature of the work done by Trinijove.
Together with the President of the Generalitat, Artur Mas, and his predecessor Jordi Pujol, the former Councillor of Socialist Education, Ernest Maragall, and the former lieutenant of mayor and current eco-socialist councillor Ricard Gom.
The current conseller of Benestar Social i Família, Josep Lluís Cleries, and the general director of La Caixa's social work, Jaume Lanaspa, also went to the grand room of the Fabra i Coats.
But he stated that Trinijove was born at a very difficult time and that the current one too is, but added that the work done so far "is a good foundation."
The president gave Trinijove as an example for knowing how to fight the bad circumstances and for giving hope to people with difficulties.
"The crisis isn't a new thing for the entity and for the work it does," he said.
Evicted an occupancy farm where macro-feasts took place
The invaders had taken over 11 of the 13 floors of the building in Balmes, 51
The 12 occupants left the estate at noon yesterday without offering resistance
Everything seems to indicate that the nightmare experienced by the residents of 51 Balmes Street has come to an end.
Dozens of people voluntarily left the property they illegally occupied at noon at noon yesterday, and where they organized macro-feasts that desperated to the neighborhood, by a court order and without the need for a police intervention.
At the end of the operation, the lock of the door was changed to prevent a repetition of the situation.
"From now on there will be an access control," the administrator said.
The okupas, who had invaded 11 of the 13 floors of the building - the rest they are inhabited - took the entire electrical installation and part of that of the water, in addition to painting the walls and leaving waste of all kinds, especially cans of alcoholic beverages.
The first three floors were the scene of the massive festivities, which lasted for up to two days and despised the tenants.
Next to the building there is a nightclub, Balmes 51, which has also been damaged.
Its owner, Maria Pantinat, explained to Europa Press that since the building was occupied the business it has dropped by 80%.
The owner of the nightclub points out that the invaders of the estate made him unfair competition, as they sold the glasses at three euros, while in the nightclub they are worth 10.
In addition, the owner of the leisure venue had had to respond to neighborhood complaints due to noise, while, he pointed out, the inconvenience is not provoked by the clients of the nightclub, but by the inhabitants of the occupy house and, above all, the attendees of the festivities organized there, who had increased their frequency and intensity in recent weeks, according to the angry neighbors of the area.
The manager of the nightclub explained that the inconveniences were so great that he even reached an agreement with the owner of the property and nine months ago "gives" the rent.
The Urban Guard also confirmed that in recent weeks it had received more complaints from neighbours about the situation.
The president of the municipal group of the PP in the municipality, Alberto Fernández Díaz, had demanded last week "immediately to dislodge the property in order to put an end to the problems of coexistence and civility that produce the neighbors, as well as the degradation of the area."
The popular leader explained that before the summer there was only one floor of the occupied building, but over the last few months the okupas had been entering all the floors of the building and in almost all the floors, and that there have even been robberies.
In this sense, he added that, "it is necessary to put an end to impunity and be blunt against the incivism that has been generated on the estate."
Also yesterday, the Mossos evicted a building in Nou Barris.
At 3:30 p.m., the police went to Mont-ral Street.35, after receiving a call from a neighbour who had observed that four people were accessing the building.
All four were arrested at the time.
Park or mountain?
Architects agree on the occasion of the call beyond controversy over fees
The municipal contest on the 16 gates reopens the debate on Collserola's role in Barcelona
In the mid-1980s, in the midst of the now extinct Metropolitan Corporation of Barcelona (CMB), this administration printed some corridors of promotion of Collserola.
The pamphlet defined the mountain as the great park of metropolitan Barcelona.
What's more, it was accompanied by a comparison, in figures, with New York Central Park.
With the death of the CMB, in 1987, the park repositioned, in the imaginary of many, such as the upper limit of Barcelona, being the Catalan capital of the few that do not orient themselves in the north-south axis but above (mountain) -below (sea).
Collserola &apos; stealth status as a natural park results in this idea.
The reference was no longer Central Park but Yellowstone, with boars on the Yogi bear's role.
These were until, in September, the city council convened a mega-competition composed of 16 contests for so many other access gates to Collserola.
A gate refers to the idea of the park, without a doubt.
Either that or someone wants to put doors on the field.
The question comes out lonely: Collserola, park or mountain?
The contest, both because of its purely organizational aspect and for what it means of reflection on the city is the topic of conversation between all the Barcelona architects.
It's almost a recurring theme to lay when two members of this former caste of elected recognize themselves in an elevator.
And this fact gives rise precisely to one of the few coincidences between what three Barcelona architects think, and university professors, who have presented themselves.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Mádol and Marta Bayona: the contest is timely.
For Roldán, after addressing the coastal front "it is necessary" to debate on "the mountain front," since Ildefons Cerd , that of green apples, has not taken place.
"It must start from the base that the most extensive park in Barcelona is the beach.
Among other things because of its great accessibility.
Collserola is more complex," says the architect for whom the title of "La Collserola Doors" encloses an "lexic trap": you don't have to think of them "like access to a park, but the delimitation of some areas where you have to act."
Modol, like Roldán and Bayonne with experience in the urban world, describes the proposal as an "intellectual challenge" with "some dangers."
Dangers derived from the "uncertain that it is today to project the public space" after the experience lived in the 1980s and that he had in the area of the Forum "its singing of the swan."
The first fear is the very delineation of borders on where it can be built.
"Limiting something is already fixing the next step," i.e. its overpass.
"We have to think about new urban, planning instruments, because the current ones [in reference to the Metropolitan General Plan (PGM) ] can give rise to real disasters."
Closer to the question, Mádol believes that, by simplifying Collserola's valley slope "it can be understood as a park, that of Barcelona, no," because of a purely topographical issue.
He recalls that there has already been a work done by Collserola's Patronat who "shouldn't fall into a broken sack," although he appreciates the opportunity that the city council offers its guild to intervene in this project "from the very beginning."
And even more, he understands that the proximity of the mountain with the city is the safe behavior that allows the architects to enter the debate, but "doubts" about whether it is this technical profile that should lead the multidisciplinary teams ("every success," he points out) that have had to be created: "The architects should only be a tool."
Bayonne insinuates that the answer to the question that gives title to this piece is complex, because in the bases of the contest there is a lack of an overall vision of Collserola: "16 transversal contests have been convened," he says, "in the mountain-seat direction, but there is no longitudinal reading, that sees and understands the area as a whole."
This implies, at least where appropriate, that he has chosen to participate in the 16 competitions.
On the other hand, a project for one of the gates can clash in program and definition with that of the architect of the door next door.
In his opinion, however, the city council should ensure that the band immediately superior to the Dalt Round should foresee, in its entirety, an area of equipment that would make a smooth transition between the city and the green.
Casino forgives Jonás Larrazábal and will be released
After a 72-strong meeting, Casino Red's legal proxy apologized to the mayor's sibling of the mayor of Monterrey for the process followed for alleged blackmail
After serving 72 days in prison, Manuel Jonás Larrazábal will be released after the legal representative of the Casino Red gave him forgiveness before the Second District Criminal Court, which follows a blackmail trial against the brother of the mayor of Monterrey.
After the attack on the Casino Royale, the owner of Casino Red, Sergio Gil Garcia, released a series of photographs and videos in which Manuel Jonás allegedly demanded the payment of an economic fee so that the Regiomontano municipality would allow the clandestine operation of the gambling house.
Upon the public denunciation, the State Attorney &apos; s Office opened criminal proceedings against the Brother of the Panista mayor and was arrested and entrenched for 30 days on September 2.
After four hours before the Agent of the Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office 3 in Patrimony crimes, he was transferred to Casa de Arraigo 1, located in the Ancient Quarter of the state capital.
He stayed there for the 49 days after being given a second restraining order after the Public Prosecutor &apos; s Office officer denied the crimes of bribery and organized crime.
On October 19, he was transferred to Cadereyta's prison for allegedly blackmailing the owners of Casino with one and a half million pesos; however, he only stayed for a few hours in the medium-security prison as there were death threats against him.
The authorities allowed him to continue the trial in the municipal cells of San Nicolás de los Garza.
On October 25 he was issued with an arrest warrant by the clerk of the Second District Criminal Court, Jorge Yánez, who said that after almost two months of investigations, sufficient evidence was checked to determine Jonah's guilt, such as the collection of 1.5 million pesos from the representatives of the betting house in exchange for allowing its operation.
On Monday, Casino Red's legal proxy, Víctor Aldo Garcia Gómez, went to judge José Luis Pecina to apologize for the royal mayor's sibling; so in the next few hours he could recover his release.
During the legal proceedings, the agent of the Public Prosecutor's Office attached to the Court, Ramiro Arias, questioned Garcia Gomez if the damage had been repaired and which of the partners of the gambling house granted the forgiveness, although the lawyer tried to reserve the name.
On the basis of file 197/2011, Jonás Larrazábal's legal forgiveness is granted and no claim may henceforth be filed regarding the process that was being followed; in addition, he will not be required for compensation for harm or punishment.
After the hearing of the legal representative of the gambling house, at about 15:50 Larrazabal Bretón was immediately released.
The clerk of the Court, Jorge Yánez, moved to the prison of the municipality of San Nicolás de los Garza to notify Jonah that he has been legally pardoned and his file will be filed.
He will also be notified of the judge's immediate release order issued.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome gives details of the plane crash where the Secretary of the Interior, Francisco Blake, lost his life and seven more people
The Ministry of Communications and Transportation (SCT) reported that the results lysecuted about the accident where the Secretary of the Interior, Francisco Blake Mora, and seven more people lost his life, indicate that the helicopter was directly impacted on the ground in complete structural integrity, without the pilot being able to attempt a forced landing.
During a press conference, Dionisio Pérez Jácome, Secretary of Communications and Transport, mentioned that before the aircraft took off, the pilot found that the weather conditions were adequate to fly.
"The elements that seem to indicate that the unit had several contacts with the terrain, the blades had contact with trees 25 meters where the unit lost the fuselage."
He also reported that the unit was designed for 19 people, so it had the necessary requirements to transport the staff.
In addition, he said that the investigations carried out in the impact area do not report the appearance of pieces on the ground.
"In a search they report that there are no pieces or components of the ship elsewhere, the impact was presented structurally.
"The design of the helicopter was for 19 commuters, it had adequate requirements to carry the passengers.
The terrain presents a slope of seven degrees to 30 degrees of inclination.
At the moment, the secretary assured that it is a priority to have the results as soon as possible; however, the investigation can last several months, it could even reach a year.
Gilberto Lopez Meyer, Director General of Airports and Auxiliary Services, said that at the time of the impact, the helicopter of the Presidential General Staff was operating in cruising speed and that this happened in ascending soft terrain.
"This information reinforces the hypothesis of a normal cruise operation at the time of the impact," he said.
At the press conference, the head of the SCT, Pérez Jácome, listed six relevant elements in the investigation:
VisVisual Flight:
According to the aeronautic regulations the flight was carried out under the rules of visual flight, this implies that the crew must have visibility during flight and terrain.
Weather conditions:
The AICM reports located adequate conditions for take-off, as the aircraft progresses towards the border area between the DF and the State of Mexico there were layers of cloudiness at low altitude.
Selected routes:
It is likely that due to cloudiness conditions the crew has searched an area of lower elevation and greater visibility towards the Cuernavaca valley.
Location of impact.
The impact coordinates and the latest radar aircraft search coincide, probably prior to the impact there was no loss of control or alteration in the flight trajectory.
Dispersion of wreckage of the aircraft in a reduced area:
The information available so far allows us to suppose that the impact occurred on a straight trajectory.
The pattern of dispersion of wreckage of the aircraft suggests that the helicopter was impacted on the terrain under conditions of structural integrity.
There has been no evidence of fire or blossom:
At the site of the accident, evidence remains undisclosed in the ship's wreckage of some kind of explosion or fire.
The latest results of the investigation indicate that he may have had multiple contacts with the terrain.
Parts of the aircraft have been found at an elevation of 9,200 feet above the middle level of the sea, the terrain presents a slope that ranges from seven degrees to 30 degrees of inclination.
The photographs show the sequence in which it is predictably dismantled throughout that tour.
The information provides elements that appear to indicate that the helicopter may have had multiple impacts on the terrain.
After the thorough search in an area larger than the cordoned-off site, around the accident, no dispersed parts of the aircraft have been found, which reinforces the presumption that the impact occurred under conditions of structural integrity.
MSS Announces Judition to Found Obesobic
He directs Social Security research to undiscover the obesity gene in children and young people; he is supported by IPN specialists
The Mexican Institute of Social Security (IMSS) announced that it directs a research to undiscover the obesity gene in children and young people, called protocol 'Genetics of obesity of childhood and adolescence', which will make it possible to have markers associated with overweight.
In a statement, the head of the Biochemistry Medical Research Unit of the Specialties Hospital of the Siglo XXI National Medical Center, Miguel Cruz Lopez, explained that a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, nutritologists and social workers was integrated.
This team is supported by graduate specialists and students of the National Institute of Public Health (INSP) and the Center for Research and Advanced Studies (Cinvestav) of the National Polytechnic Institute (IPN).
In the research the population is invited to participate, whether or not they are entitled, to go to the participating sports units of the IMSS in the Valley of Mexico, where the tests are carried out for the minors, every Saturday from 8:00 a.m.
He emphasized that the protocol began in the Cuauhtémoc Sports Unit (State of Mexico Poniente) and that from October 8, the activity has been carried out in the Nezahualcóyolt Sports Unit (State of Mexico East).
Upon the achievement of the objective of gathering 500 or 600 minors, the study will continue in the Independence Sports Unit (south of the Federal District) and finally in the Morelos Sports Unit (in the north of the Federal District).
He explained that the purpose of this research is to know the components and the importance of genetics with regard to the development of obesity and to identify in Mexican children and young people risk factors to prevent the emergence of chronic degenerative diseases.
Upon the celebration of World Diabetes Day on 14 November, he specified that the objective is to bring together 3,000 children and young people between the ages of six and 14, with the consent and supervision of their parents, for the implementation of specific food questionnaires.
They will also be asked about physical activity and inherited history of diseases, with the aim of avoiding risk factors that trigger conditions such as diabetes or kidney failure.
He explained that on Saturdays an anthropometric measurements are applied to all participants (thream, size, waist circumference), blood pressure, determination of glucose levels, triglycerides, cholesterol and insulin and genetics (the aim is to identify genes associated with childhood obesity).
Cruz Lopez pointed out that this investigation is due to the concern of health professionals about the alarming weight gain in minors.
'The health surveys of 1999 and 2006 indicated that in just six years there was an increase, in the case of boys, 77 per cent in obes obesity, and 47 per cent of girls; in terms of overweight there was also a dramatic rise,' the IMSS specialist said.
He indicated that the justification for the search for markers is based on the fact that the country's population differs from others because of its genetic history.
He reported that, on average, the Mexican has 65 per cent U.S., 30 per cent European and five per cent African and this condition is reflected in genes that are associated with diabetes and excess weight.
He explained that, in general, obesity genetics results in the inability to burn the excess energy we consume (calories) and that is stored in the form of fat.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit for the participants is the comprehensive evaluation of the children, which makes it possible to detect if they present any degree of overweight or obesity.
Even, he said, the presence of obscure-colored folds (acantosis) in the neck and forearm, which indicate the probability that he will suffer from metabolic alterations and even prediabetes.
The information is handled in a confidential manner, the doctors deliver it in writing to the parents, who are explained each of the measurements and changes in the lifestyle they have to make to prevent diseases.
Cruz Lopez explained that there are three main components that cause overweight, obesity and other chronic conditions: sedativeism, which is the lack of daily physical activity, stay most of the day sitting in front of the TV and using means of transport rather than walking short distances.
It is also due to the high caloric intake, i.e. the increased consumption of sugary, carbobohydrate drinks (altites, bread, pizza, hamburger, etc.) with elevated content of fats and proteins and the genetic that is analyzed in this protocol.
Asphalt of UNAM would avoid bacquakes
Rafael Herrera, a researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry of UNAM, develops modified asphalts with polymers to produce a mixture with better performance, regardless of environmental and temperature conditions
"If the signs sent by the pavement were met, it wouldn't be necessary to repair about 200,000 potholes every rainy season in the Metropolitan Zone of the Valley of Mexico, warned Rafael Herrera Nájera, a researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) of UNAM, who said that if the process of construction and maintenance of an asphalt pavement is adequate, the potholes could be reduced in quantity or even eliminated completely.
In his lecture A technological opinion of potholes, dictated within the framework of the International Year of Chemistry, announced that the lab in charge develops modified asphalts with polymers to produce a mixture with asphalt-like characteristics, but with greater mechanical resistance, both at highs and at low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera estimated that this type of modified asphalt could be used in places where the climate is extremely hot, such as some cities in the north of the country to improve their performance and, therefore, the formation of potholes.
He explained that the asphalt, the heaviest part of the oil, is a set of hydrocarbon molecules, some with a high molecular weight, called asphalt, and others of low molecular weight, called malthens, that together give the pavement its characteristic viscoelastic behavior.
The viscoelastic behavior of the asphalt is that at high temperatures, about 180 degrees, it behaves like a liquid, at 120 degrees Celsius its viscosity is very high and at 50 degrees Celsius has an almost solid state, while at very low temperatures, the asphalt becomes brittle, he added.
This means that in the streets the resistance of the asphalt is not uniform, but is modified during the day according to the environmental and temperature conditions, the specialist in chemical engineering warned.
The FQ researcher observed that the streets of Mexico City are mostly covered by flexible pavilion, which consists of six layers of materials, of which the last three involve the asphalt.
Among the most shallow layers is the asphalt folder, made up of small asphalt-bathed stones and then compacted.
"Every one of the stones that constitutes the folder," he said, is bathed with asphalt.
When the vehicles are transited and efforts are generated, it allows the forces to spread very well on the pavement.
If it were a modified asphalt they would spread even better, the chemical engineer said.
On the asphalt folder, he abounded, is placed one last lacquium called rolling folder, which is made of a thin stone material, i.e. an arena that is also swimmaged on asphalt.
Because it's the layer that's gonna be in contact with the tires of the vehicles, it must have more mechanical strength than the asphalt folder and provide friction along the way.
Since being the part of the pavement you are going to receive and disseminate the efforts, the rolling folder also requires having the right thickness and you must take care of the material from which it is made.
The climate, especially the humidity and the variations in temperature, are also a very important factor to consider, especially the water, he said, because it significantly affects the performance of the asphalt folder.
When the terrain gets wet, the water diffuses inside the folder and it's difficult for it to come out, this causes a softening of all the layers of the pavement and therefore the formation of potholes.
In the process of forming a bump, he said, the first to wear and tear is the bearing film and only then the asphalt folder is affected, but the damage doesn't occur instantly, but it's a process that the first signals with proper maintenance programs.
The member of the National System of Investigators also explained that the vehicular charge is another important factor in the formation of potholes and, therefore, the thickness of each of the layers of the pavement must always go in relation to the vehicle load and ensure that it is fulfilled, to prevent bumps from forming frequently.
Rafael Herrera warned that there's still plenty to investigate and work on asphalt, for example, asphalt emulsions, which in other countries are already on the market and make it possible to repair potholes easily and without the need to use high temperatures.
What's the Valley candidate lacked to be New Miss Colombia?
Analyzing the factors that influenced Melina Ramírez Serna not reaching the crown of Miss Colombia.
The Valley gets its 14th Princess title.
The Cauca valley is left with the desire for Miss Colombia's wreath.
This time Melina Ramírez Serna had to settle for being on the podium of winners, to wear the silver wreath and the band who accredit her as the new princess of Colombia.
With the result obtained last night in Cartagena, where Miss Atlantic rose with the wreath and scepter, the department already adds 14 titles of Princess and Melina join the legion of Vallecaucan women who have been close to the wrath but were proclaimed princesses including Miriam Ospina Benoit (1947), Clara Domínguez Borrero (1949), Patricia Bellini Ayala (1979), Lorena Álvarez (1981), Rose Mary Alzate (1983), Olga Maria Arenas (1987), Leila Blanque (1988), Mónica Evers (1989), María Consuela Pinedo (1990), Diana Isabel Romero (1993), Giselle Garcés Aljure (2000), Catalina Giraldo (2007), Stephanie Garcés Aljure (2008).
Today the great question that many are asking themselves is what was missing?
Because the result represents the defeat of a great favorite who in each presentation showed that she had everything to be the new Lady Colombia, not in vain was chosen Queen of the Police and Best Face Jolie of Vogue, titles that obtained queens such as Taliana Vargas or Natalia Navarro who took the crown of Miss Colombia.
The first factor that is considered key in the decision taken by the jury to appoint her First Princess is that he did not convince with his answer to the question "What is that book that has marked you and why? made by Martín Murillo Gómez of the Literary Carreta Leamos.
To the interrogatory the vallecaucana replied:
"I read a lot, I think a lot in the literature, I think it's a way of culturalizing, I definitely believe that the book that marked me in childhood is "The Prince," it's a pretty deep book.
Currently I read "Crime and Punishment," it is a book of universal literature that we must all read; and nowadays I am reading the book of Irene Nemirovski, a Russian, a very beautiful book that is called "Lord of Blood.""
An unapnezed answer first for the term "culturalizing" and second for not explaining in a concrete way how each of the books he cited have marked it.
But at the end of the ceremonies Melina expressed feeling calm and happy with the result "the universe is wise happens what has to happen.
And to all the people, please read a little, "The Prince" is a great book".
Among the hypotheses released it is said that Valle was burned by favoritism.
A Bogota journalist hinted that being the favorite, the ovated and the nominee who wore all the preliminary prizes (Oster cuisine rethircy, vote of the public for the best artisan costume, and Reina Madre), "that awakened the jealousy of the other candidates, stressed the queen and that's why she didn't shine on the stage."
And that was noticed in his first swimsuit presentation on Hilton beach and two parades on the coronation evening.
Although both looked safe, it was noticed that he lacked the strength and impetus that the representatives of Magdalena and Atlantic did demonstrate.
Ariel Osorio, reporter of the RCN Channel program "Like at Home," said the fact that Melina was appointed princess "becomes that the candidate who earns the most awards during the contest does not look like queen."
In addition, Valle showed the last very elusive with the press, and there he was wrong.
Another hypothesis is that Valle did not manage to convince the jury about his interest towards social work, which is ultimately the essence of the National Beauty Contest.
Two days before the election, international juror Lucy Doughty said they were searching for a woman committed to the social needs of her country.
"Beauty alone isn't enough in Miss Colombia, you have to look beyond the physics and focus on her social sensibilities, that before entering the Contest they are already involved in projects," he explained.
It is also speculated that Valle sinned because of his defects in derriere and fibrosis noticed in his abdomen, but it is contradictory because precisely the jury chose her as the candidate 'Natural Beauty' with the healthiest body, good nutrition and responsible physical work.
Renters preparer Jorge Hernán Orozco recognizes that Melina didn't have the best body, that there were better, "but her problem of fibrosis in the abdomen, that the truth for me wasn't very notorious, dropped points on the last day."
Meanwhile the journalist Guido Hoyos of Cromos magazine considers that Vallecaucana's error were the surgical interventions that took place.
Surgeries are okay, but Valle couldn't do it.
His body was never his forte and here they charged it to him."
For this award, which for the first time presents the National Beauty Contest, after the parade in a swimsuit arose a cruel prediction that was celebrated last night, as it was said that it was said that Raymundo conceded to Melina so as not to give him the taste of the crown.
Reality or not, the truth is that Melina from today begins a year of reign, not as a sovereign, but as a princess, a position that gives him the possibility to represent Colombia in one of the international competitions by designation of the National Competition itself.
However, she will go down in history as one of the queens who, despite her overwhelming passage through Cartagena, was dodged the wreath.
This was the work of the caleño who identified Alfonso Cano's corpse.
A Lophoscopist from Cali went down in history as the man who confirmed the death of "Alfonso Cano."
The work of these officials is key.
On November 4, a lophoscopier of the CTI of Cali went down in history as the man who confirmed the identity of the main head of the Farc: "Alfonso Cano," through the collation of his fingerprints.
Of the seven lofoscopists who work in the CTI's section, that this week it was up to Eliazar Gonza Gonzalez, a man from Puerto Tejada who has been working for 21 years in the area of judicial investigation.
At the beginning of his career, Gonzalez worked in the municipalities of Chocó, where he often had to intern for more than five days in the jungle to get to the place where the corpses that had to be identified.
In 2000 he moved to the capital of the Valley and, after carrying out the corresponding courses, he specialized in lofoscopy, which is the study of friction skin, i.e. the palms of his hands and feet.
In the week of the operation against alias Alfonso Cano to Eliazar was warned to wear minimum clothes for three days.
Something great awaited him.
On Friday night he left for the Cauca in a helicopter from Palmira.
He knew that he had to identify two corpses and that it was suspected that one of them was the man who directed the oldest guerrillas in the world, so he was asked to wear Guillermo León Sáenz &apos;
However, when he arrived with the research team to the place where the body was located, he did not recognize it because of his physical appearance, because it was very different from what he had seen in the media.
He was unbeaten, dressed like a peasant in the area, without glasses and much thinner.
But the footprints don't lie.
Accompanied by a Galtonian magnifying glass, ink, a transplanter and a lantern, Eliazar compared the fingerprints of the corpse to those of the card.
His eye didn't miss: it was "Alfonso Cano".
At the same night, the man from Puerto Tejada was in charge of confirming the Minister of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, that he was the top head of the Farc.
Searching the footprint
According to a Dactiloscopista de la Sijín de Cali the recognition made by President Juan Manuel Santos to Eliazar Gonzalez, at the press conference for the operation against "Cano," is very significant.
A lot of people don't know about our work.
Dactiloscopy is an exact science, with no margin of error, that can become key in the resolution of crimes.
In addition to the identification of corpses, lofoscopists support investigations into crimes such as burglary and homicide and confirm the identity of the people captured.
When they are capted, many criminals have fake ballots or don't have papers. They don't have papers.
We'll get the flickering print and search them in the database.
The researcher said that, in a theft case, it was up to him to remove a piece of wall from a house, because in that place the thief had leaned his hand to remove a safe full of jewellery and money.
"The walls are difficult because they are not suitable surfaces to make a fingerprint, but in that case we were able to get the fingerprint of the whole hand and identify the offender, who had participated in different thefts," he recalled.
Footprints may also rule out or reassert suspects in homicide cases.
They recently found a dead man with bottles of liquor around him.
Through the investigation it has been determined that a family member could be a suspect of the crime and, indeed, a fingerprint on the bottle coincided.
Even if this doesn't guarantee he's the killer, he does put him at the crime scene.
A CTI dactloscopist explained that in the objects "the fingerprints are removed with chemical or physical reasgents."
When they appear, you use a transplanter or tape to lift them.
After this, the impressions are sent to the Asif, which is the criminal database, or to the Registry.
"The problem is that there are still many undocumented Colombians," said the CTI expert.
He clarified that not everything is as shown on CSI's television show.
Just as not everything that shines is gold, not everything that is touched becomes a mark.
However, if it can be used, if the respective match is done.
The "outraged" defy authorities in New York
Hundreds of protestors crowded into Zuccotti Square for camp.
A large part complied with the eviction order and moved to a nearby paruqe.
The members of the Occupy Wall Street movement defied the New York authorities on Tuesday as they tried to re-enter, under the protection of a court order, to Manhattan Square from which they were evicted in the early morning and when trying to occupy another park in the Big Apple.
Hundreds of protesters clashed in front of Zuccotti Square, where they wanted access thanks to a court order that allows the "outraged people" to re-encamp on the site, while another part of the movement responded to the eviction occupying a park further north of the city, where there have been further arrests.
"Our idea is, if the court doesn't allow us back camping in the square, we're going to settle somewhere else and we will continue the legal battle to return to Zuccotti, as well as through peaceful sit-ups and demonstrations," said a spokeswoman for the "Occupy Wall Street" movement Mark Bray.
Bray detailed that the "outraged" response to evicting their camp personally dictated by New York Mayor Michael Bloomberg reached the courts, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order prohibiting the forcible evacuation of the site and allowing protesters to remain encamped.
In addition, the "indignados" proceeded to occupancy of a new park located at the confluence of central Canal Street and Sixth Avenue, very close to the entrance to the Holland Tunnel, linking New York and New Jersey, and owned by the parish of Trinity Church.
For at least an hour they managed to occupy the place, but then the police came and proceeded to arrest some of the protesters, although Bray was unable to confirm the specific figure, while telling Efe that he saw several journalists who were also handcuffed.
The organization assures that there are several hundred people who had settled at that point, where a delegation of religious leaders has already been displaced in support of the members of the movement and has tried to mediate with the police.
In the meantime, Zuccotti Square - which is a privately owned enclosure - is still closed pending the holding of a judicial hearing scheduled for this same Tuesday in which the legality of the evacuation this morning must be analyzed.
Some of the protesters displaced there carried and distributed copies of that court order at this time and even handed them over to the agents guarding the square, which caused "some small clashes," Bray said, who denounced that some officers beating the protesters.
The court order prohibits the authorities from evicting Occupy Wall Street members or applying "laws that have been published after the occupation began or to prohibit protesters from re-entering the park with tents or other properties they previously used."
Mayor Bloomberg indicated to the press that, due to this judicial order, the city had suspended the reopening of the space to the public and the protesters, whom he warned, however, that local laws do not allow them to re-settle with tents and sleeping bags.
The regent personally defended the eviction carried out early in the morning of Zuccotti Square, an operation in which about 200 arrests were carried out and which it was decided because the protesters were "violating the law."
Bloomberg explained that he gave the order to evacuate the square because it was becoming "a place where people do not come to protest, but to violate the laws and, in some cases, to hurt other people," since "some businesses have received threats" and the neighbors fear "because "because of their quality of life."
"The eviction this morning was disgusting and shows that Bloomberg is basically more interested in preserving the financial interests than those of the American workers," added the spokesman for the movement, who accused the mayor of "disrespect for freedom of expression."
"Occupy Wall Street" officials also indicated that they plan to organize "great actions" by Thursday in collaboration with community organizations and trade unions, to commemorating the two months of the protests, which began on September 17.
Pamela Anderson Will Be the Virgin Mary in a Christmas TV Special
Together with Michael Bublé and other outstanding Canadians, he will appear on the television show "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1.
Canadian Pamela Anderson has been Tim Allen's assistant in "Home Improvement," a rescuer on "Baywatch," "Playboy," the wife of rock musician Tommy Lee and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary in a Canadian television Christmas special.
The private television channel CTV announced today that Anderson, along with the "crooner" (balist) Michael Bublé and other prominent Canadians, will appear on the television show "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1 giving life to a very particular Virgin Mary.
CTV today distributed footage of the television program in which Anderson, also known for the wide distribution on the Internet of a pornographic home video in which she appears along with her then husband, the drummer of "Mitley Cr?e," Tommy Lee, appears dressed as the Virgin Mary holding a doll that represents Jesus in a manger.
At the scene, Peters, a renowned Canadian comedian of an Indian-born man who recently acted as a presenter in part of Charlie Sheen's "My Violent Torpedo of Truth" tour, appears as Joseph.
In addition to Anderson and Peters, CTV's Christmas special features the Canadian singer Bublé, who at the end of March married in Buenos Aires the Argentine model Luisana Lopilato, and who is no stranger to the controversy.
Bublé recently called American Kim Kardashian "shocking" during one of his concerts in New York.
Japan Reborn After Earthquake
GDP grows by 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption and puts an end to the spiral of falls that began in March
Since last March the Japanese economy has been paralyzed, still convalesced by the devastating earthquake that occurred in a nuclear crisis, the worst catastrophe suffered by the Japanese country since World War II.
The Tokyo Government &apos; s first estimates put material losses at $300 billion, a blow that, despite the difficult global context, appears to have mitigated.
After three consecutive trimesters of falls, the Gross Domestic Product data between July and September served to turn the page, definitively: GDP increased by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and by 6% in an annualized rate.
The one recorded in the second trimester - for the Japanese, the fiscal year begins in March 2012 - also represents the fastest rate of growth from January to March 2010.
The recovery is explained, in part, by the rebound in the purchase of machinery to restore areas ravaged by the earthquake and subsequent tsunami.
In fact, companies increased their investments by 1.1%, compared to the 0.9 per cent decrease in capital expenditure observed from January to March, the first decline after six months of uninterrupted hikes.
However, the true revulsiveness of the Japanese economy does not rely solely on the Keynesian impulse that calls to take advantage of the crises to strengthen - or rebuild - the infrastructures, use the shortages to grow from scratch.
Japan has awakened, above all, thanks to the evolution of domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60 per cent of Japan &apos; s GDP.
This variable reached 1 per cent, boosted by increased spending on vehicles, travel and leisure activities.
The stigma of the yen
Tokyo has entrusted itself to the internal market, when the yen - the Japanese currency - has become one of the "housing values" chosen by investors fleeing the debt crisis in the euro area and murky U.S. forecasts, a privilege that weighs as a slab in the accounts of exporting companies.
With a louder currency, products are more expensive abroad, a serious setback for a country whose economic flogging line is determined by the health of large tech groups.
Toyota or Sony, for example, blamed its weak results between July and September on the yen's quotation.
While the markets are continuing its siege of the peripheral countries of the EU, Yoshihiko Noda's government does not lower its guard.
"We must be attentive to the remaining risks, such as the deterioration of some foreign economies, the impact of Thailand's floods and the rapid appreciation of the yen," the Secretary of State for Economic Policy Motohisa Furukawa said yesterday.
Indeed, the prospects are not encouraging.
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) said the majority of member countries showed sharper signs of an economic slowdown in September.
The indicator anticipating the turning points in the global economy showed that the main powers are slowing down.
It dropped four-ten-tenths to 100.4 points, up from 100,8 in August.
The euro area was even below-average, which stands at the level 100, with eight tenths rushing to 99.13 points.
Athens feeds the doubts again
Papadi says the adjustments will be approved, but conservatives won't sign any written commitments
With peripheral risk premiums skyrocketing and uncertainty reinvigorated in the patches, Greece is still entangled in the maze that leads to an exit from Europe's major debt crisis.
Feeling that the "consensus" of the new Greek transitional government is more imposed by the demands of Brussels rather than the genuine conviction of the political forces is beginning to be confirmed with facts.
While the new Prime Minister Lukas Papadimos warned yesterday that there is no alternative for the structural reforms and that the 100-day period agreed in his appointment may fall short to implement them, the leader of the Conservative New Democracy party, Antonis Samaras, assured that he will not support new austerity measures in any case and reiterated his demand for new elections for the scheduled date, February 19.
"The main task of this government is to implement the decisions of the summit of 26 October," Papadimos said at the opening yesterday of a parliamentary debate that will culminate tomorrow with a vote of confidence to the new Executive.
A package containing 24 programmes to promote employment, the reform of the disciplinary code of civil servants and new measures against tax evasion will be the first initiatives.
However, Samaras' message doesn't cleude the doubts about her position: "We are committed to helping the transitional government, but we haven't committed to anything else."
The Conservative leader was a step further in his pulse towards the EU and the IMF, the agencies that must unblock the 8 billion Athens urgently needs, and made it clear that he will not give his approval to any letter of commitment to implement the adjustments.
But the European Commission, still echoed because of the impact caused by Papandreu's ordeal, reiterated yesterday that its demand is firm: it will not unblock the aid if the new government and the leaders of the main Greek parties do not sign "a written compromise without ambiguity and as soon as possible."
The spokesman for Economic Affairs, Amadeu Altafaj, explained that troika inspectors will return "very soon" to Athens to discuss with the new government "what is needed to pay the sixth tranche" of 8 billion, although there is also no definitive date.
The Greek sketch still has thread for a while.
Doña Sofía: "They are instruments for progress and hope"
La ReQueen stands out the "preponderant" place of Spain in relation to this type of finance
Spain "has reached a leading place worldwide in the role of Microfinance."
The Queen Sofia recalled yesterday that the country has become in the last two decades the second donor of the proposal in relation to this intrusion of cooperation, whose "essence" lies in being an "instrument for social progress, justice and the hope of a better future for all of humanity, based on confidence in the human being."
The Queen inaugurated yesterday the 5th World Microcredit Summit in Valladolid with the conviction that "there is no doubt" that these types of operations are "absolutely indispensable" in order to achieve the Millennium Goals established by the United Nations with horizon 2015, and, "very especially, in which it aims to halve the volume of people who live with incomes below a daily dollar, in which the "threshold of poverty is fixed."
Spain "continues to work" to expand the network of beneficiaries "to all corners of the planet, and assumes this task with "responsibility," said Doña Sofía, who hoped that the initiatives and contributions that originate at the Summit, which will last until Thursday, "ensuring the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest, and above all for women," the main beneficiaries of these loans.
There are "a lot of the achievements achieved" and "many of the challenges we face."
The Microcredit Summit arises not only as a forum for discussion, but also as the platform from which to promote "two basic goals."
Thus, Her Majesty explained that by the end of 2015 there will be 175 million poor families that have accessed these financial services and that a hundred million such households leave the poverty line at which they are plunged.
The impulse in the conception of microcredits in Spain and in the rest of the world "has followed the path started years ago in a pioneering way" by Muhammad Yunus, highlighted Doña Sofia as an "an act of justice and due recognition."
He described the father of this tool as an "admirable idealist in the universal cause of the fight against poverty," whose work he has known for 15 years, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to know on the ground her lending policy.
This experience was followed a year later by the first World Summit in Washington and other meetings such as those held in Ivory Coast in 1999, Canada 2006, and "everyone I have been able to see the continuous evolution," explained Her Majesty, whose link with this project yesterday earned him the qualification of the "family leader" assembled in Valladolid by the "baker of the poor."
Finally, the Queen Sofia concluded her speech by encouraging those present to participate in the debate and in the search for solutions to advance the fight against poverty.
"Ethical Imprative".
The Minister of Foreign Affairs, Trinidad Jiménez, warned that solidarity "is an ethical imperative" for every democratic state.
In this regard, he stated that it would be a mistake to link the cooperation with developing countries to periods of economic bonanza, since it is a "permanent obligation."
Jiménez considered that micro-credits represent "a serious and rigorous policy dedicated to eradicating poverty," and now "more than ever" the governments of developed countries must work under criteria of solidarity and do not allow it to fall victim to the cuts.
For Spain, micro-credit consists "one of the main lines of development cooperation."
For her part, the Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodriguez, applauded the "success" of giving "confidence" the people "excluded to whom everyone closes the gates"; and the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, welcomed those present to a city that "blows its doors infected with the spirit of solidarity."
The CDU proposes to elect the European President by universal suffrage
On the first day of the congress of the Christian-Democratic Union (CDU), in Leipzig on Monday, November 14, in Leipzig, has passed without great difficulty for Angela Merkel, Chancellor and President of the Christian-Democratic party.
Europe &apos; s future had not initially been included on the agenda, but the issue prevailed in the autumn, in particular to satisfy the militants who believe that the government's policy is currently too unsuitable with the Greeks and it could in the future with the Italians.
The motion concerning the Presidency of the European Commission, proposed by the leadership, was almost unanimously adopted.
Of the 1,001 delegates, there were only nine congressional votes and 10 abstentions.
The multi-minute ovation ovation that was won, with their interventions, Angela Merkel and, after several hours, Wolfgang Schuble, the Europhile Minister of Finance, clear any possible doubts.
The important idea of the Chancellor, and of the motion, is summed up in a motto: the answer to the crisis "is no less Europe, but more Europe."
Not only because Europe supposes peace, but also because "nine million jobs depend directly on the euro."
For the chancellor, "it's time to make its way into Europe."
This could be manifested by amendments to the Treaty of Lisbon to strengthen the control of the European Union over the budgetary policies of the countries of the euro area, although it could also result in the election of the President of the European Commission by universal suffrage to "give Europe a Face."
In any case, that is one of the proposals of the motion adopted by Congress, clearly an idea by Wolfgang Schuble.
Undoubtedly it is unlikely that the twenty-seven will quickly reach an agreement on this radical change: Great Britain is hostile to it and the leaders of the CDU seem to know that Nicolas Sarkozy too.
But the ball is on the ground.
On the other hand, the delegates have rejected by an overwhelming majority the proposal to weigh the weight of each country of the directive of the European Central Bank according to the weight of each economy, which would obviously strengthen Germany's weight.
An eager to soothe the mood within her party, the Foreign Minister has increased references to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl. But she has not tried to win its congress at the expense of the "small" European countries or struggling countries willing to criticize the arrogant Germany.
Although the motion on Europe dedicates a paragraph to the importance of "French-German friendship," Angela Merkel hasn't lied to France.
Except in passing.
Remembering that, two centuries ago, a bloody battle took place in Leipzig.
In fact, it was one of the most humiliating defeats for Napoleon's army.
The incidence of sick leave fraud
After the subsidies, the UMP and the government are launching an offensive against sick leave.
Nicolas Sarkozy will be in Bordeaux on Tuesday, November 15, to visit a box responsible for family subsidies and deliver a speech on the subject, already recurrent since 2007, of the fight against social fraud and aid.
In addition, in order to accompany such an offensive, that it starts the will to save rigorously, but it still remembers an axis of the right-wing presidential campaign, most of them want to focus on disease casualties.
In the first place, to implement budget cuts: the Government plans to impose a fourth-day-of-the-jobs on private-sector workers before they can take the drop.
Ultimately, unless there is a specific enterprise agreement, a worker who falls ill would lose four days &apos; wages, i.e. a minimum of 15 per cent of his monthly income.
It's a measure that could lift a wave of rage but would save 280 million euros, according to Les Echos.
In addition, it would be accompanied by another measure with a more symbolic scope: to impose on civil servants one-day absence without remuneration in the event of a leave of work (so far, they have no shortage, compared to the three days applied in the private sector).
While these issues are being debated, the majority also put the issue of speculage on the table, this time oriented towards abuses of labour casualties.
"Controversal controls are going to be considerably increased and, in addition, if someone is found committing a fraud, they will have to reimburse the amounts," Xavier Bertrand said, speaking to RTL on Sunday, November 13.
As in the other cases, the effects of the advertisement seem slightly disproportionate to the reality of labour-relief fraud.
In fact, since 2002, this issue has been constantly being monitored and a number of political offensives have been launched that have normally borne fruit.
The French don't get sicker more than in the rest of Europe.
Let us look closely, first and foremost at the national figures.
According to the French Institute of Social Security (CNAM), more than EUR 237 million was paid in occupational leave benefits in 2006.
According to the French Institute of Research and Documentation in the Health Economics, subsidies for casualties accounted for 5 per cent of health spending in 2008, amounting to 11.3 billion euros.
Of this amount, 46 per cent corresponds to maternity and occupational casualties, while 54 per cent are related to sick leave, amounting to Euro6.7 billion.
As a result, 2.5% of health expenditure.
In 2010, the French took an average of 14.5 days of leave of work, compared to 17.8 in 2009, according to a study by the Alma Consulting group.
An an average that undoubtedly hides deviations: another survey, published in 2007 by Syrian professional management Monster.com, showed that 75 per cent of the 40,000 French earners surveyed said they did not take a single day of sick leave.
Another study, in this case of Assurance Maladie, indicates that in 2010 37% of sick leave lasted less than 8 days, 22 per cent between 8 and 14 days, 15 per cent between 15 days and a month, 15 per cent between one and three months and 11 per cent were longer.
By comparing them with our European neighbours, French workers are no more likely than in other areas to catch sickness casualties.
A long-running study carried out in 2010 by two CNRS researchers indicated that, between 1994 and 2001, the overall non-existence rate (for both health and other reasons) ranged from 10 per cent to 11 per cent, compared with 20 per cent to 28 per cent in Denmark, 15 per cent in the United Kingdom or between 16% and 18 per cent in the Netherlands.
Fraud in sick leave is not particularly significant compared to undeclared work.
Fraud has quite limited scope.
Reinvigorating the legislation, since 2002, has caused controls on sick leave for more than 45 days to become systematic.
In 2008, out of 1.5 million inspections, CNAM found 13 per cent of the 285,000 carried out that short-duration casualties were "unwarranted or too long," i.e. 37,050 cases.
In the case of casualties of more than 45 days, subjected to systematic inspections, 11 per cent of cases were considered to be "duly adapted or unwarranted; a figure with a total of 1.2 million, amounts to 132,000.
As a result, a total of 169,000 "unwarranted" cases are reached among several million workers who took sick leave.
Undoubtedly, an insignificant figure by comparison, for example, with black work.
According to UMP Member Dominique Tian, the author of a report on this issue in June, this practice would result in losses of tax revenues of between 9,000 and 15 billion euros a year, i.e. more than the total cost of casual subsidies and, without a doubt, considerably more than fraud in the latter area.
Papademos: Greece must implement a new adjustment plan
Greece will need a new adjustment plan to strengthen its economy.
This is what Prime Minister Lucas pointed out before Parliament on Monday, 14 November; he also added that the one hundred days that have been granted to his Union Government would not be sufficient to achieve such an objective.
"To make every effort to recover the economy, we need the support of our European partners and a new budget adjustment program," said the Head of Government, who delivered his first public speech at the inauguration of the debate on the vote of confidence in Parliament on Wednesday.
The implementation of the decisions taken during the Eurozone summit on 27 October will be the "main task" of the new government, as "it is at stake" the country's stay in the euro area, he added.
As a result, Papademos has predicted that the country &apos; s public deficit will be reduced "to around 9%" of GDP by the end of the year, after reaching 10.6 per cent in 2010 and 15.7 per cent in 2009.
The Right Rejects New Authen for Austerity Measures
Antonis Samaras, the leader of the right-wing group, on Monday expressed his support for the measures already approved to try to emerge from the debt crisis, but has warned that his party will not commit to additional austerity measures.
"We won't be voting in favour of new measures," he said during a meeting with the deputies of his "New Democracy" party.
He pointed out that he was in accordance with the objectives of reducing the deficit and the debt, as well as the fight against wasting, but contrary to any policy that prevents economic recovery.
Olli Rehn, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs, who warned that the IMF and the EU wouldn't unblock the 8 billion-euro loan needed without a written guarantee of support from all parties for the planned measures, Antonio Samaras has replied that his word would be enough and that he wouldn't sign anything because of external pressure.
An expert committee says euthanasia and assisted suicide should be legalised
Candies live in denigration of death, Royal Society of Canada report says
With regard to palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health-care institutions and doctors to work together to ensure the best palliative care, and not only in cancer cases.
Canadian society lives in denial of death.
In fact, only 9% of Canadians are willing to talk to their doctor about the conditions in which they want to die and make arrangements to that effect.
This is one of the conclusions of an imposing report by the Royal Society of Canada, with the participation of six experts from various fields, including ethics, legal and physician.
This committee advises the Government to amend the Criminal Code in such a way that assisted suicide and euthanasia is permitted, when the patient raises this option and has been tried competent to make the decision.
To begin with, the pundits cite a recent survey by the Economist Intelligence Unit that compared the quality of death in 40 countries around the world.
Although Canada occupies its 10th place, the study points out that "the medicalization of death in Canada has created a culture in which people are afraid to deal with the issue of death."
On medication or not, 77 per cent of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, according to experts selected by the Royal Society of Canada.
And, even though the majority of Canadians want to die at home, 68.6% of them end up dying in the hospital.
Overall, the highest rate in this regard is Québec, where 86% of the deaths occur in the hospital.
With regard to the sensitive issue of euthanasia and assisted suicide, the report &apos; s authors reached conclusions that do not coincide with the federal Government &apos; s position, which has already stated that it does not wish to amend the Criminal Code in relation to euthanasia and assisted suicide.
However, according to the experts, who have also studied the situation in all the countries where these practices have been legalized or do not constitute a criminal offence, there is no evidence that the fact that they are not considered a criminal offence has did not cause an abuse of its application, i.e. the use of euthanasia or assisted suicide with non-competent persons or who have not given their consent.
Jocelyn Downie, co-perpetrator of the report, said yesterday by teleconference that, on the contrary, there were more excesses in those countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide have not been legalised.
Euthanasia is practiced in Canada, although it is clearly illegal."
"After-assisted suicide is practiced in Canada, although it's clearly illegal," explains Jocelyn Downie.
Candians, in favour of euthanasia
Remember that 85% of Canadians have already spoken out in favour of euthanasia, as they believe it would allow people whose death is close to alleviating their suffering, according to an Angus Reid survey conducted in 2010.
In addition, 66 per cent believe that legalizing euthanasia will not suggest that the lives of elderly or disabled people have less value.
With regard to persons who had engaged in assisted suicide, 41 per cent of respondents were of the view that proceedings should not be initiated.
With regard to the maintenance or interruption of health care to a patient, the situation is not so clear, according to the authors of the report.
In addition, among its recommendations they suggest indicating in the Criminal Code that the withdrawal of health-care, if legally rejected, does not constitute a criminal offence.
The expert committee is also calling for health professionals to be trained in the duty to respect the fact that a patient refuses end-of-life care without fear of facing a complaint.
With regard to palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health-care institutions and doctors to work together to ensure the best palliative care, and not only in cancer cases.
Ultimately, with regard to palliative sedation, which is already widely used in Canada's health institutions, the committee asks that, unless it serves to reduce physical pain, it be considered euthanasia and is subjected to the same procedures.
Other pundits express reservations
Other experts in the field of health law have already expressed their reservations regarding the findings of the expert report of the Royal Society of Canada experts.
Pierre Deschamps, specialist in the law of health, has described as "extreme" the position of the experts who sign the report, among other things because the practice of euthanasia is not limited to patients in the terminal phase.
"This leaves the door open to a situation in which someone who is tired of living and is over the age of 18 can ask for help to commit suicide," says Deschamps, who yesterday consulted a summary of the report.
Mr. Deschamps also points out that the authors of the report appear to prioritise personal self-government over any other value of society.
However, he adds that "in society there are tags and limitations."
Health law specialist Margaret Somerville also voiced "strong opposition" to the report's findings, which she described as a "demonstration for euthanasia."
Margaret Somerville, who also belongs to the Royal Society of Canada, calls into particular question the lack of abuse in certain countries where euthanasia and assisted-assisted suicide are legal, such as in the Netherlands and Oregon.
The King of Jordan urges President El Assad to leave power
Damascus denounces a "plot"
Syria is increasingly isolated
The partisans of Bachar El Asad demonstrated yesterday before the building of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Yesterday the diplomatic pressure was intensifying on the Syria of Bachar El Assad who, with the support of Russia, resists it and denounces that there is a "plot" against the country.
Two days after the suspension of Syria by the Arab League, the European Union has tightened sanctions against Damascus, while Turkey and Jordan neighbouring Syria have spoken out in favour of President Assad's abandonment of power.
Jordan's King Abdala II yesterday was the first Arab leader to ask President Bachar El Assad to "abandon power" in Syria, where a quarantine of civilians and members of regime forces has already died.
"If Bachar really worries about his country, he should resign, in addition to creating the necessary conditions for a new phase of Syrian political life," said the King of Jordan, Syria's neighbouring country, in an interview for the BBC.
For his part, the head of Turkish diplomacy, Ahmet Davutoglu, has lamented that Turkey's mediation efforts since the beginning of the year with the Syrian regime have ended in failure.
"Those who are not at peace in the Middle East with their peoples and can't satisfy them will depart," he said, alluding to Syria, a neighboring country with which Turkey is taking an increasingly tough stance.
For its part, following the decisions of the Arab League and of the European Union, the United States has celebrated "strengthening consensus against Assad and the actions of the Syrian regime."
"The international community, the United States, the EU, the Arab League and other countries, such as Turkey, are adopting an increasingly tough tone" regarding the crackdown in Syria, State Department spokeswoman Mark Toner said.
In the meantime, 16 civilians and at least least 19 members of the regime's forces have already lost their lives in the Deraa region of southern Syria, as announced by the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights (OSDH).
Two other civilians have died "during bombardments and heavy machine-gun shootouts" in the besieged city of Homs, according to that NGO.
During a press conference, the Syrian Foreign Minister, Walid Al Mualem, considered that the country "advances towards the end of the crisis."
In addition, it has reacted strongly to the decision of the Arab League to suspend Damasco from his duties, which in his opinion constitutes "a dangerous step."
Syria "wouldn't falter," he added, ensuring that "trashed plots against Syria will fail."
Following the decision to suspend Syria, the Arab League is currently considering a "protection mechanism for the protection of civilians" and wishes to send 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, media and military observers to the country.
French Foreign Minister Alain Juppé has also expressed his support for sending U.N. observers to help protect civilians in the face of the repression of the regime which, in his opinion, lies in "paranoia."
It is envisaged that a new extraordinary meeting of the League will be held tomorrow in Rabat to develop the implementation of the crisis exit plan adopted on 2 November, which envisages, in particular, the withdrawal of the armed forces from cities in the hands of the opponent and the release of detained demonstrators.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov yesterday considered the decision of the Arab League as "incorrect," while China urged Syria to implement the exit plan of the crisis, although it did not express its support for the implementation of possible sanctions against Damascus.
- Italy
Monti asks the markets for time and sacrifice to the Italians
The future head of the Italian government, Mario Monti, has asked the markets for some time to form his team and to launch a program that will include "sacrifices" aimed at regaining the credibility lost in recent months of the Berlusconi government.
The former European commissioner debated with the political parties yesterday and today he met with the trade unions and the bosses.
It should form a relatively restrained cabinet, made up of technocrats outside Parliament.
"Monti has talked about an extensive program with numerous sacrifices," said Francesco Nucara, a member of one of the abundant small parliamentary groups that participated in the meetings, having met with the President-designate's Council.
For his part, the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, has stated that he expects Mario Monti to request before Friday a vote of confidence from Parliament to ensure that the new government has sufficient support.
The consultations will result in Mr Monti &apos; s presentation of a limited list of a dozen ministers to the Head of State Giorgio Napolitano.
Monti has commented on the nervousness of the stock markets and the bondholders yesterday, asking them for time.
In the "democracy, there are precise deadlines" for forming a government and preparing its program, he stressed, adding that he is "sure that the markets will have patience and understand it."
His appointment by the president was initially welcomed from the markets, but concern was later raised, particularly after an unprevious drop in industrial production in the euro area in September.
The Prime Minister-appointed should create a team made up mainly of technocrats, even if he has stressed that he would like to include "politicians."
The new government will have to operate until 2013, the date of the upcoming legislation, he believes.
According to the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, the new government will obtain a vote of confidence from Parliament before Friday.
Monti has pledged that, when he takes office, he will work in the face of the urgency and with the aim that Italy "recreates its prominence" in Europe.
A spokesman for European Commissioner Olli Rehn stressed that "Even with a new government, it will not change our diagnosis of the Italian economy."
The EU, convinced that Rome will not achieve its objective of achieving a budgetary balance in 2013 despite the austerity plans adopted in recent months, has called for further stiff measures.
Italian "chief of the bosses" Emma Marcegaglia, who will see the killing, also insisted on the need to boost the economy, "because a country that does not grow cannot respect deficit requirements."
Monti, renowned for his competence and his independence as European Commissioner (1994-2004), represents a "change of epoch" for Italy after 17 years of "berlusconism"; the "Professor" symbolizes "the challenge of seriousness" and "another Italy," according to the publishers.
The great unchain will be the longevity of his team.
President Napolitano wants to avoid an early election, since Italy has to place about 200 billion euros in state obligations before April 2012.
A Russian scientist has collaborated with the Iranian nuclear program
A Russian scientist, Vycheslav Danilenko, has collaborated with Iran in the development of a detonator that can be used with a nuclear weapon, an American NGO specializing in atomic risk said on Monday.
To identify the researcher, the Institute of International Science and Security (ISIS) is based on a recent report by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) and other documents issued by that UN agency.
Last week the IAEA expressed its "serious concern" about the "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear program.
According to ISIS, based extensively on documents from the IAAF, Danilenko, born in 1934, reportedly worked for three decades since the 1960s at a Soviet military nuclear center in Tcheliabinsk (Oural) and would have been involved in the manufacture of Synthetic Battery by Explosion.
In 1989 or 1991, he would have abandoned the center to form a company focused on the production of "nanodiamonds" in Kiev.
His company &apos; s economic problems would have prompted him to get in touch with Iran's embassy in Ukraine in 1995.
He is then believed to have co-operated with the Iranian program between 1996 and 2002, before returning to Russia.
In its last report, the IAEA mentions that there are "clear indications that Iran is developing" a nuclear detonation system "with the help of a foreign expert, who not only knew the technique well, but also, as reported by a Member State to the Agency, he worked for most of his career in this technology in the military nuclear program of his country of origin."
Mur crimes and crimes against Muslims have increased by 50 per cent
Crimes and crimes against Muslims have increased by about 50 per cent between 2009 and 2010, while the remaining types of racial and religious violence have decreased or increased only slightly, according to statistics released by the FBI on Monday.
According to these figures, the total number of acts of violence committed against Muslims has increased from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, an increase of 49 per cent, compared to a 13 per cent increase in acts of violence against Catholics, a 4 per cent reduction in crimes against Jews and an overall 14 per cent increase in the number of anti-religious crimes.
The total number of "hate crimes" has increased slightly to 6,628 cases, of which 47.3% is due to racial differences and 20 per cent to religious differences, according to the FBI.
"After a decline in 2009, it is troubling that these types of crimes and crimes have increased again," said human rights organization Human rights organization Human Rights, "the increase in acts of violence againstMuslims is particularly significant," the organization added in a statement.
"Human Rights First has long pointed out that violence against Muslims, as well as all types of hate crimes, should be seen as a serious violation of human rights," adds head of the organization, Paul Legendre.
"The U.S. government can and must strive to put an end to these abuses," he adds, noting that such efforts could include an improvement in police reports of "hate crimes."
"Hatred crime" in U.S. federal law was inherited from the era of the civil rights struggle.
This legislation was adopted after the death of Martin Luther King to punish acts of violence linked to race, skin colour, religion, origin and now sexual inclination.
Twitter changes its platform and looks more like Facebook
The microblogging site Twitter on Monday modified the presentation of its platform to allow its users, in particular, to observe the activity of the people they follow, which causes their service to reappear a little closer to that of Facebook.
A new "activity" tab and another so-called "name" allow the activity of other "Twittos," i.e. those netizens whose messages ("tweets") to be more legibly and live.
This makes it possible to know if they follow up messages, if they are subscribed to new accounts, if they call a favorite a message, etc.
This new Twitter formula was tested "with a small percentage of users in August" before spreading it to the rest of the network on Monday, Twitter spokeswoman Carolyn Penner told AFP.
Twitter, which was known for its instinctive operation, with slightly esoteric terms (RT to forward a message from someone else, followfriday pour recommander de suivre un autre utilisateur) faisant le bonheur des initiés, s'ouvre ainsi to a public plus great avec maniement plus comparable to celui des réseaux grand public Facebook et Google.
On the network, users have suddenly debated "tweets" with different opinions the interest of this innovation.
"It is useless, it is invasive and looks too much like Facebook," he writes.
"The Activity feature is going to help me to better follow some elements of my Twitter stream," he writes .dannykronstrom.
- Austria
Introducing a "goldiary rule" in the Constitution
The Government of a large Social-Classian-Class-Classian coalition that is in power in Austria has decided on Tuesday to include in the constitution a budgetary "golden rule" to reduce the public deficit and thus avoid a possible degradation of Austria's sovereign note by credit rating agencies (triple A).
It was the Social Democratic Foreign Minister, Werner Faymann himself, who announced this decision after a Council of Ministers, when the Austrian public debt reaches 74.6%, a figure much higher than the 60% ceiling set by the Maastricht Treaty, but clearly inferior to the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"If Austria &apos; s solvency were reduced by a single level, from AAA to AA, we would have to pay an additional 3 billion euros in interest each year," said Democratic Christian Foreign Minister Michael Spindelegger.
Austria thus responds to a request of the Brussels Summit to the member countries of the euro area on 26 October: in it, the Heads of State and Government requested the inclusion, before the end of 2012, of the reduction of the public deficit and the recovery from the balance of public accounts in the Constitution or a law with equivalent value.
The mechanism adopted by the Austrian government is approaching the "German model," since Germany was the first European country to adopt such "rule."
Prior to 2017, the structural government deficit should decrease each year by 0.75 per cent of gross domestic product (GDP) and, starting in 2017, the structural government deficit of the federal state should not exceed 0.35 per year of GDP, while the regional states, as well as communes, should present balanced budgets.
The objective is that, by 2020-2021, the public debt will be reduced under the 60% criterion set by the Maastricht Treaty.
A loud yen
Unfailed threatens new turnaround market intervention
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that the Japanese authorities would re-exaggerate the marking of the change if the yen continued to increase.
"We'll intervene, just like we did the last time, if we check that there's over-volatility" in the yen's exchange rate, Noda told the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen climbed to levels close to the historical highs that produced the intervention of the Japanese authorities on October 31.
On that day, Tokyo massively sold yen for dollars to reduce the value of the Japanese currency, which had just reached a new record since 1945 compared to the U.S. currency.
But the yen has been gradually regressing since then, driven by purchases from investors who consider it a "fifted value" in these uncertain times of global economic slowdown and debt crises in Europe.
The dollar re-listed on Tuesday at less than 77 yen and the euro has fallen below the sympathetic 105 yen bar, reaching levels close to those caused by the last intervention.
Prior to the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar was quoted at about 120 yen and the euro at more than 160 yen.
This blossom of the Japanese currency harms exports of the archipelago, as it boosts Made in Japan products and reduces the value of revenues earned abroad by Japanese companies when they are converted to the national currency.
"Japan is struggling to rebuild itself" following the earthquake and tsunami of March 11, devastated the Tohoku region (northeast), Node said, which fears that the shaft of the yen will cortege the weak recovery.
The world's third-largest economic power has regained growth in the third quarter (1.5 per cent compared to the previous quarter), for the first time in a year, thanks to an increase in exports and consumption of households affected by the disaster.
But the Prime Minister believes the high yen levels did not reflect Japan's "economic bases."
Finance Minister Jun Azumi has asked the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which on Tuesday inaugurated a two-day meeting of its Monetary Policy Council, to take all possible measures to weaken the yen.
However, the deputy governor of the institution, Hirohide Yamaguchi, warned that the BoJ had already taken "appropriate measures," implying that greater monetary flexibility should not be expected on Wednesday.
During its last meeting at the end of October, the issuing institute increased by five trillion yen (47 billion euros) its purchases of Treasury Letters, bringing to 55 trillion yen (519 billion euros) the threshold of the funds it dedicates to the acquisition of various financial securities to flood the liquidity market.
Euro area:
Guys grow, bags falter
The risk of contagion of the debt crisis continues to harm European indices.
In spite of the appointment of new heads of government in Greece and Italy, as well as better growth figures in France and Germany, the rates of the most weakened countries continue to rise.
Still concerned about the stability stability of the eurozone, European stock exchanges plunge a little further into the negative zone on Tuesday, November 15.
In Paris, at 10 a.m., the CAC 40 loses 1.4 per cent to 3,064 points.
The Dax loses 0.4 per cent in Frankfurt to 0.4 per cent and London by 0.1 per cent.
"Based on new tensions over loan yields from the worst-hit euro area states, stock markets around the world continue to yield some of their recent gains."
"Investors, nervous, are well aware of the risk of contagion," explains Terry Pratt, an institutional runner at IG Markets, as cited by the Reuters news agency.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange has closed with a 0.7 per cent fall, resurfacing the concerns of market controllers about Europe's ability to contain the debt crisis.
"Japan, such as the rest of the world, has a fixed eye on Europe and exchanges will continue to take place at a reduced margin of variations, so investors will have no evidence that the situation has stabilized," says Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
Reappearance of worries about the future of the eurozone hit the single currency, which is still down on Tuesday.
The euro remains at its lowest level for a month and a half.
Around 10 a.m. the European currency was worth $1.3570, down 0.4 per cent.
Runners say it has lost value after Chancellor Angela Merkel's party, the CDU, which met in congress on Monday, adopted a motion that foresees, in particular, the option of a struggling country leaving the eurozone without leaving the European Union.
Italian and Spaniard rates far exceed 6 per cent
The lending rates of Italy and Spain remain at very worrying levels.
On Tuesday morning, the return on 10-year Italian bonds reached 6.85 per cent, while Spanish securities at the same time offered 6.2 per cent.
These tensions over interest rates in the countries of the south, but also of France, continue to fuel the fears of the interveners.
The head of the future Italian government, Mario Monti, has asked the markets for time to improve the situation in Italy, which risks suffocating with the weight of its debt.
The new Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos, for his part, has stated that the implementation of the decisions taken at the eurozone summit on 27 October would be the "main work" of his government.
Spain is in the spotlight of the markets, while the legislative elections will be held there on Sunday.
The difference between 10-year bonds in Germany and Spain has reached an all-time high.
Waiting for euro area growth figures
From a statistical point of view, France recorded economic growth of 0.4 per cent in the third trimester in the third trimester, a more favourable figure than the projected 0.3 per cent, driven by domestic consumption.
The Insee, however, has revised the second-trimester figure downwards, with a 0.1 per cent reduction in gross domestic product (GDP) rather than stagnation.
In addition, the fourth trimester is what really worries economists.
Germany, for its part, has recorded an increase of 0.5 per cent over the same period.
Early forecasts are expected for the eurozone as a whole at 11 a.m., after the European Commission has warned about the risk of a new recession.
Energy policy:
the facts must be worth more than the dogmas
If there's an issue you cann't deal with short-term considerations or simply thinking about emotion, it's energy policy.
The availability and the cost of electricity directly influence the purchasing power and the competitiveness of companies.
It is fitting that an electoral meeting is the time to reflect on such issues and the place of nuclear energy.
Discussion will only be useful if you look at all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The French must base their opinion on objective facts, on rational data.
Now that there are 7 billion people on the planet, the demographic evolution will mean the doubling of electricity demand by 2050, unless a huge part of the world's population is deprived of a vital element.
At the same time, the growing shortage of fossil resources will have to be alleviated in order to continuously produce electricity, not forgetting the urgent need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions in order to combat climate change, an issue that has disappeared from the ecological debate.
The Fukushima accident doesn't modify any of these parameters.
That's why Germany stands alone in its decision to renounce nuclear power.
That is not the case in Belgium, which puts hers to the need to find a fountain capable of replacing it, or from Switzerland, which only aims to build state-of-the-art power stations.
On the other hand, Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India and Brazil, to name a few examples, are still resolutely moving forward with their projects.
The U.S., meanwhile, after Fukushima decided to add a unit to its park, resuming the construction of an interrupted power plant following the Three Mile Island accident.
If the German model, based on renewable energy, is sometimes presented as an example, the reality goes against such a speech.
Berlin's choice is going to lead to an increase in the price of electricity, increased energy dependence due to the increasing need for imported gas, in particular from Russia, an increase in CO2 emissions due to the construction of gas and coal plants.
In a few months, the harmful effects will be noticed.
Consideration should be given to the case of Denmark.
Champion of Europe in the wind sector, of which it obtains 30 per cent of its electricity, it is also one of the large users of coal and gas due to the intermittentity of the wind; for this reason, its CO2 emissions and the price of its electricity respectively are 65 per cent and 50 per cent higher than the European average.
On the contrary, thanks to the constancy of our energy policy, our neighbours pay an electricity bill 40 per cent higher than that of French households.
While in a globalized economy the cost of energy is a key factor in competitiveness and a guarantee of the maintenance of industrial utility on the national territory, French companies benefit from the cheapest electricity in Europe.
For this reason, any significant reduction in nuclear energy would mean a sharp increase in the price of electricity, which would detract from the marvellous statements about the re-industrialisation of our country and the defense of our purchasing power.
It's a price that the vast majority of the French refuse to pay and, in fact, do not initially express opposition to this type of energy, according to the polls.
Let us remember that the energy policy of our country has lost the construction of an industrial sector with 125,000 director jobs and 410,000 indirect jobs.
Widely oriented to export, it covers a galaxy of SMEs specializing in international markets that can create non-dispossible jobs.
If France were to decide to abandon nuclear energy, it would mean the sacrifice of a large part of these SMEs and the loss of 6 billion euros a year in French exports of nuclear equipment and services.
Which electrician would command an EPR designed to operate sixty years, when the collapse of its manufacturer is planned?
In order to solve the nuclear energy equation, it must be admitted that there is no miraculous source of electricity, just as there is also no diabolical source.
Areva &apos
In the case of nuclear energy it is essential to maintain the maximum level of safety.
In France, we already have a security authority that exercises strict and transparent control over existing power plants and on the construction of Flamanville.
Our teams of renowned professionalism are also obsessed with security.
In the middle of the century, the nuclear sector has experienced three serious accidents.
Only one on Three Mile Island occurred under normal operating conditions; it didn't cause consequences either for people or the environment.
In Chernobyl, the accident was due to the design of the reactor and unforgivable human failures.
In Fukushima, some people seem to forget that the accident was due to two natural disasters of an unprecedented scope, resulting in the death of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear sector will learn from this accident, just as it did of the previous two.
Thanks to this progress and cooperation between the security authorities, and the French and German operators and builders, EPR presents a design capable of resisting such phenomena.
While it is studied by all the electric players who want to build a power plant, the interruption of its construction in Flamanville would be a great gift for the competitors of Areva and EDF, since it would leave them free to assume the leadership in the inevitable development of the nuclear sector.
It is indispensable and legitimate to look for the best energy options for our country, but it would be irresponsible to let excitement, dogmas and partisan maneuvers dominate such an important debate for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Immigration, a burden or an opportunity for the economy?
The immigration policy implemented by Nicolas Sarkozy has been criticised from various points of view: the saturation of the police, judicial and administrative services subject to the policy of figures, the compatibility of this policy with the self-proclaimed status of "human rights country" in France.
More recently, it's administrative bullying of foreign students that has made headlines, with the Minister of the Interior (see Libération newspaper of 23 May) proclaiming last that France "doesn't need foreign talents, workers or waiters."
However, it is seldom analysed from an economic point of view.
The point is that, on this issue, there is quite a lot of consensus between the left and the right.
In the case of the right, Jacques Chirac established the guidance by stating in 1976 that "900,000 unemployed should not be a problem for a country that has about two million immigrant workers," while on the left, Michel Rocard tone explained that France "can't take care of all the misery in the world" in 1990.
The only difference, the degree of generosity
In 2005, both the left and the right shared the fear of the invasion of the national territory by a multitude of Polish plumbers.
On both sides, immigrants are seen to be a burden on France's economy and society.
The only difference is the degree of generosity with which they act with regard to immigrants.
In his program, Francois Hollande merely includes banalities, indicating that co-development will make it possible to solve the "immigration problem."
This idea of immigration as a burden, a problem, is perhaps profitable electorally, but economically it becomes very expensive.
As economists Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron point out in a recent work summarizing the most recent knowledge on immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.), there is broad consensus among specialists on the positive impact of migration flows on economic growth, wages and employment in countries receiving immigrants.
The limitation of immigration weakens growth and hurts employment.
Fears related to the impact of immigrants are based on the idea that they can replace national workers, in particular low-skilled workers, by pushing to reduce their wages.
But the experience shows that, in reality, immigrants are rather complementary than substitutes for national workers and mainly perform professions in sectors with a lack of employment.
Unskilled immigrants work in sectors that don't attract national and most skilled workers in dynamic sectors where training doesn't meet the demand for staff.
A positive effect on wages
Just as a surgeon would find it difficult to work in a country that lacks anaesthetists, complementarities between nationals and immigrants cause the latter to have a positive impact on the wages and employment of nationals.
Given the above, Giovanni Peri estimated that a 1% increase in migratory flows represents an increase in long-term real wages of between 0.6 and 0.9 per cent.
And, without taking into account the fact that the diversity that immigrants contribute contributes to the creation of ideas and economic growth (there is a large proportion of naturalized U.S. Nobel laureates and companies such as Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Immigrants are also net contributors to the social systems, since they contribute an average of 1% of the total budget in European countries.
The International Labour Organization calculates, for example, that in Germany an immigrant arriving at the age of 30 will make a net contribution (income less expenditure) of 150,000 euros to public budgets on average throughout his or her life.
At the beginning of his mandate, Nicolas Sarkozy wanted to go looking for the growth points he lacked "with his teeth."
These teeth, which deterred the immigrants, had exactly the reverse effect.
Europe invented democracy, it only lacks to think of a European democracy
Democracy is a lovely but also fractious.
The European continent should be able to remember this since, although it was invented in Athens more than two thousand years ago, throughout the 20th century it has suffered the tragedies of the World War, totalitarianisms, the Holocaust, the gulag, but also to Franco in Spain, to Salazar in Portugal, the colonels in Greece.
Some might have believed that, for democracy to triumph definitively over its enemies, it would be sufficient to exercise the vote and in the most direct way possible.
We know, from Napoleon "The Little One," that it was something thought.
Universtial suffrage, although it is a necessary condition for democracy, is not sufficient on its own and can even become an "instrument of oppression," as Republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot previously said.
Yes, democracy requires much more: a public space, a public spirit, values, an organization, a separation and a control of powers, an education, Lights, economic and social solidarity, justice.
The crisis that we are experiencing in Europe is an economic crisis, but it is about to become a political and democratic crisis.
The palinady of the real-false Greek referendum had, in this regard, a double revealing effect: it showed the need for a powerful return from the political sphere, but also the worrying feeling of its impossibility, of its inconsistency.
This ambivalence must be reflected in order to overcome it.
The truth is that the front lines of the democratic battle have now shifted and that, in many cases, Republican soldiers are no longer progressing in the right direction.
We won't be winning if we demand a succession of national referendums, organized according to the needs and in a messy way.
A blank signature to the absolute European monarch or a vacuum tall of the nationalist hole: what options for the citizen.
No, we don't want to strike, but a common, full-fledged, quiet and continuous exercise of options and democracy, this time at European level.
There is no crisis in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal (in fact, we wouldn't know where the list end), because there's only one crisis, the crisis in Europe.
The totality of Europe lends itself to the orders of the markets and rating agencies that suffer from its impotence and non-sympathetic solidarity.
It's the whole of Europe that suffers humiliation at the international level.
We are concerned about the transfer of sovereignty to Europe, but it is the whole of Europe that slowly loses its sovereignty and, with it, each of its nations.
The most urgent thing is for people's sovereignty to regain the helm of the European project.
Because only together Europeans will be able to reduce the weight of their debts, free themselves from the markets and prepare their future through investments.
The exit from the crisis will require decisions at European level that cannot be taken without total democratic legitimacy, except to lead us to the disaster.
Having fought for a long time against this proposal by the socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone is now calling for European economic governance.
So be it.
But to think of it without greater political and democratic integration, and without it being lived as such by the citizens, it would be craziness and it would lead us to a new stalemate.
We need to assume a new stage of the construction of Europe.
To Build the Republic in France, it was necessary to "make" Republicans.
Recipient-motivated Revolution wouldn't be enough.
This lesson is still applicable.
We must build a more economically integrated Europe.
It can only be achieved by building a more democratic Europe at the political level, a true European people's sovereignty.
In order to build Europe, let us make Europeans.
"Contactless" chip chip cards are generalized
The global c-chip-card lounge kicks off on Tuesday in Villepinte, near Paris, with an optimistic atmosphere.
In fact, the year 2011 should end with an 11 per cent increase in global card sales with microprocessors (bankcards, SIM-card phones, etc.), with a total of 6 billion units, according to Eurosmart cabinet estimates.
The most powerful segment is non-continuing technology, which makes it possible to pay for common transportation or purchases simply by bringing the card closer to a reader.
This year 460 million c-chip-free chip cards will be sold around the world, $320 million more than the previous year.
Its share should continue to increase in 2012 (580 million units) and represent about 10 per cent of the global c-chip card market (6,925 million cards).
Europe, which has long lagged behind the Asia-Pacific region, is gaining ground in the non-contact payment sector.
Around 26 million Visa bank cards already have this feature, which is accepted at 75,000 payment terminals.
In France, its beginnings are more modest: 400,000 contactless Visa cards are being used.
But the large surfaces work by four.
Carrefour this year has issued 2.5 million Pass MasterCard non-contact cards.
In order to take the upper march, however, it is necessary that the banks make them available to their clients.
"There are two major French establishments that have committed themselves to systematically spreading in the future, among their customers, bank cards equipped with the contactless system," announces Gérard Nébouy, CEO of Visa Europe France.
The banks prove mobile phone payment
But it's only a first step toward the payment of the future: the mobile phone.
At present, there are few devices equipped with NFC (Near Field Communication) technology, which is essential for conducting transactions.
"They're lagging behind".
"But I should finally experience a real boom next year".
"We anticipate that between 80 and 120 million units will be sold in 2012 around the world," explains Marc Bertin, president of Eurosmart.
But the ben'ts take the lead.
Crédit agricole Bank announced yesterday the launch of a test between December and next June in Caen.
The iPhones of around 200 customers and collaborators will be equated with a cloth that allows contact-free payment.
The BPCE has a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
And Société générale has also stated yesterday that it is preparing the commercialization of an offer that will allow its customers to pay uncontact through its mobile phone.
From the Sahara to Nigeria, the terrorist front is on the rise
Paris worries the links between Al Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb and the Boko Haram sect.
Ties between the Al Qaeda terrorist group in the Islamic Maghreb (Aqmi) and Boko Haram's Nigerian sect, which has long been suspected, have been confirmed.
On Sunday, Algerian Deputy Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel even stated that Algiers already "has certainty."
"The way both organizations operate and intelligence reports show they are co-operating," he explained, without providing further details.
Paris seems to come to a similar conclusion.
An investigation into the abduction of two French nationals in Niger last January would have resumed the catwalks.
Kidnapped in a Niamey restaurant by people related to Aqmi, Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory died the next day along with several of their kidnappers during an operation launched by the French special forces.
In the wreckage of the intervention, French and Nigerian investigators unveiled several phone chips that belonged to the terrorists.
Terror axis
The study of the calls made indicates several interlocutors based in Mali, Niger and Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), two numbers especially grasped the interest of the researchers: one leads to a Nigerian who remained for a long time in Maiduguri, a city in northern Nigeria, the cradle of Boko Haram, and the other to a man who is allegedly close to Aqmi and the sect.
'It's progress, but we must certainly wait to have more formal evidence before talking about operational co-operation between Aqmi and Boko Haram.'
"But it's possible".
"We've known for years that the cult is enjoying external financial and intellectual support and is becoming increasingly active," says Kunle Amuwo, a Nigerian researcher.
In recent recent months, these "supports" had proved increasingly evident in the evolution and discourses of Boko Haram or in its operational modes, which now involve the use of increasingly complex kamikazes or explosive devices in the same style as those used by Aqmi.
The international community, starting with Washington, has since been pressuring Abuya to take into account Boko Haram &apos; squeeze the problems generated by Boko Haram.
The Nigerian authorities, in fact, have long considered the group as a community of enlightened uninterested.
Fashion success
Founded in 2002 in Maiduguri around a mosque, a school and fundamentalist imam Mohammed Yusuf, Boko Haram (which means, in Hausa, "Western education is a sin"), incites the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this poor region, the association is experiencing a rapid success.
Strict and demands strict respect for Shasma, while its militants hurling into the fight against the unholy central government.
The latter are oriented in the first place to the churches, the bars, the administrations.
In 2009, Boko Haram's militants were particularly active and attacks on police stations multiplied.
Abuya &apos; s backlash was, as is often widespread.
The army attacked the city and 800 people were killed.
Mohammed Yusuf, arrested in life, is killed in prison.
Since then, the cult has become radicalized and abandoned its fiefdoms from the north.
Last Christmas, he claims an attack that causes 80 deaths in Jos and, later, another in Abuya.
In June, Boko Haram re-attacks Abuya, in particular the guarded police headquarters, with a suicide attack, the first of its kind.
In August, the United Nations local headquarters became its first international target.
The creation of a terrorist axis in Africa, stretching from Mauritania to Somalia through Nigeria, worries all security specialists.
While Aqmi has attacked Mali and Niger and al-Chebab Somalis operating in Kenya, Boko Haram emerges as a new threat in the center of the mainland.
Over the past week, during a meeting in Nigeria with President Goodluck Jonathan, Alain Juppé warned him against Boko Haram and said he was ready to "share all the information."
The World According to Republican Presidential Candidates
With regard to China, with Iran and with the help of Israel, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, and Rick Perry compete in simplicity and a lack of knowledge.
An America weakened by the crisis, can it manage a complex world with simple or even simplistic ideas?
Listening to the Republican candidates for the presidential elections, voters might think that's right.
For a few days, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and the other nominees have increasingly made thunderous statements and shortcuts on foreign policy, traditionally a Republican's strength.
Lanches spread, often infused, about China, Iran, aid to Israel.
Mitt Romney, the best-placed candidate in the race for the nomination of the "Great Old Party," has threatened to take the Chinese to the WTO and denounce them "for manipulation of their currency," a key issue in an America exasperated by the migration of entire fragments from its sector to Asia.
"It doesn't matter if that implies the explosion of a trade war," he said.
Jon Huntsman, Obama's former ambassador to China and the only nominee who has a sophisticated view of foreign policy, almost drowned in the face of an attitude that "flauntes" emotions.
He has called for a firm but constructive dialogue with Beijing (Obama's current position).
But Huntsman, who squeezes in the depths of the polls, is still inaudible.
Reagan Style
Romney has also vowed a military intervention in Iran if the sanctions fail to disrupt his nuclear program.
"If I'm elected, I'll stop Iran," he boasted.
"If Obama gets re-elected, they'll have the bomb".
He also promised to increase military aid to Israel, accusing Obama of having failed his duties in relation to that partner.
Entrepreneur Herman Cain, the second highest-ranked nominee, shows embarrassing disgrace of such issues.
Recently, he urged to counter China's military threat because Beijing "attempts to develop nuclear capability," seemingly unaware of the fact that China already had the military atom since 1964.
Texan Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and wants to intervene in Iran, says he is willing to deploy the U.S. military in Mexico to fight drug cartels.
The use of torpeal torture against alleged terrorists, banned by Obama in 2009, is also advocated by Cain, Perry and Candidate Michele Bachmann.
Distincations are sizable with 2008 Republican nominee John McCain, a foreign policy heavyweight.
Opponents of the current president say that Reagan also had simple ideas and won the Cold War.
They recall that Obama himself was an amateur who had to loosen the piston in the area of counter-terrorism.
They rightly insist on the failure of their naive dialogue with Iran, the precipitous departure of Iraq's "boys" and their misrepresentations in Libya, and label it weak.
But the attacks don't make it easy for them.
The population is more positively judging Obama's national security balance, which wields Bin Laden.
From Iraq, it distrusts the military interventions that generate a sink.
And, on the grounds that it is necessary to economically rebuild America to resurrect its leadership, Obama gives the impression of being fairer.
Nord Stream, new gateway for Russian Gas in Europe
The Nord Stream pipeline, which directly communicates Western Europe with Russia via the bottom of the Baltic Sea, has already been put into service.
It must supply gas to meet the needs of 30 million households.
Gazprom, the Russian gas giant, has wanted to celebrate the event with great pomp.
Last Tuesday, more than 500 guests, including four Heads of State and Government, participated on the shores of the Baltic, between the orchestra's music and a delicate buffet, in the inauguration of Nord Stream, the new pipeline that communicates directly, under the sea, Russia with Germany.
Symbolically, German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitri Medvedev, French Prime Prime Minister Francois Fillon and his Dutch counterpart, Mark Rutte, have rotated together the steering wheel of the valve that controls the entry of Russian gas into the Western European network.
With this new pipeline, an additional 27.5 million cubic meters of Russian gas will arrive each year in Western Europe through a first had 1,224 km that connect the St.Petersburg-Pomerania Land with the Mecklenburg-Pomerania Land.
A second tube should be completed at the end of 2012 which will supply a total of 55 billion cubic meters of gas, i.e. enough to cover all French consumption.
In addition, there's already talk of the possibility of building a third tube on the same route.
According to Gazprom, the completion in time of this huge work presents an air of victory.
Poland and the Baltic countries had steadfastly opposed the project.
In fact, Nord Stream will make it possible to supply gas directly to Germany, by avoiding the terrestrial passage through third countries (Poland and Ukraine).
Suddenly, these two countries lose some of their power: it will be more difficult for them to influence Russia by threatening to close access to markets in Western Europe.
At the height of the debate, Warsaw did not hesitate to compare Nord Stream as a "new German-Soviet pact," in reference to the alliance between Hitler and Stalin to occupy Poland in 1939.
In other words, whether the issue is politically sensitive.
Western Europe, supporting the Russian project, has gained greater security of supply.
But the price is a weakening of the Europe of energy, given that the project has created a gap between Europeans in the east and in the west.
In just five years, Russia has been able, in just five years, to triumph its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and wealthy Western countries, large consumers of gas and with increasingly limited reserves.
"This pipeline constitutes a component of our association with Russia, a new artery that unites us organically," Francois Fillon confirmed during the inauguration of the pipeline, demonstrating the extent to which the big contracts of the gas sector are as political as they are economic.
All this explains the wide smiles on the faces of Gazprom's directors, numerous in Lubmin, at the point of arrival of the gas.
The construction of this pipeline also represents a technical achievement.
The submarine pipe is made of cement-coated steel to be protected from the anchors of ships.
It rests at the bottom of the sea, at an average of 200 meters deep.
To build it, it was necessary to avoid minefields dating back to World War II.
"It was necessary to remove or destroy a hundred of them, as a precaution," says Jens Moller, spokesman for Nord Stream.
It was also necessary to increase measures for the protection of fauna and flora.
The tube is a long metal snake that weighs like 242 Eiffel Towers and is not interrupted in all its length: neither by valves, nor by maintenance stations.
A control rig was planned in Sweden's waters, but those responsible for the project relinquished it when they saw that the Swedes weren't very willing to allow a Russian state-owned company to settle permanently at sea off its shores.
"In the event of a breakdown, we will interrupt the passage of the gas in less than a minute," Jens Moller says.
In the event of leak, the gas from the pipeline will therefore be released into the atmosphere.
But the builders consider it uncommasible for a leak, as they have predicted a leak every 100,000 years.
The control of the state of the pipeline is guaranteed by a submarine robot, dragged by a ship, carrying out external inspections, as well as through a device circulating inside the pipeline.
In the form of a cigar, it is inserted inside the tube, which has a diameter of one metre, and moves with the gas.
Upon his arrival, after three full days of travel at a speed of three meters per second, he provides precise data on possible deformations of the metal.
The pipeline has been designed to operate for fifty years without the need for any repair.
The circulation of the gas is guaranteed simply by the pressure.
At the very beginning it is 200 bars and, at the exit of 100 bars.
Five companies have joined forces to be able to carry out this project.
Gazprom is Gazprom, which owns 51 per cent of the shares.
It was joined by German E.ON and BASF (15.5% each), as well as the Netherlands Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (9% each).
These five companies have created a joint venture, called Nord Stream, in charge of building and exploiting the pipeline.
It is headquartered in Zoug, Switzerland, where taxation is particularly favourable.
Gazprom then rented to this company, for a period of twenty-five renewable years, all gas transport capacities.
"Gazprom will be paid for the total capacity, regardless of the amount of gas that is actually transported," says Jens Moller.
The financing of the construction of the pipeline has been carried out by 30 per cent with the contribution of the shareholders and by 70 per cent through bank loans.
The total cost of the work should reach 7.4 billion euros for the two tubes.
For GDF Suez, for example, the investment has reached 240 million.
But minority shareholders are confident they won't exceed their investment, as Gazprom alone assumes the risk of expenses.
The pipeline emerges from the Baltic in Lubmin on a sandy pine-poddered shore.
Valuations, 4.5 meters tall, form the exit gate of the gas.
There are sensors that measure the pressure, temperature and specific characteristics of the gas on arrival, before it is transferred to the German pipelines.
As of last Tuesday, the terminal receives a million cubic meters of Russian gas every hour.
In total capacity, it will be three million per hour and in 2012, six million.
- Moleskine, a trade notebook.
Created half a century ago by an Italian company, Moleskine wants to give people a taste for scripture.
He's got an air of adventure, hours of writing in the light of a candludle.
The Moleskine notebook is easily recognized, with its black rectangular pastes, its gum, its broken-wheel-coloured blades, as time-aging.
Moleskine notebooks with the current appearance, however, were born in 1997.
The Italian Mode Mode company, based in Milan, at that time decided to launch a notebook of design notes, but distinguished.
María Sebregondi, at that time consultant to the company, was in charge of the release of the new product.
The notebook is inspired by an old production technique, the mole skin (topo skin), which refers to varnished cotton that was used, among other things, to cover the banquets.
This type of notebook was highly valued by writers and travelers.
"Moleskine has adapted to current taste an old and forgotten object, an icon of the avant-garde artists until the 20th century," says Maria Sebregondi, who has since become responsible for the brand in Mode Mode.
And he achieved success.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales increased by 26 per cent to a volume of 12.7 million units sold.
The confidential production of the first few years has been left, in which only 3,000 notebooks were designed.
Moleskine goes beyond Italy's borders and exports its products to 70 countries, from France to the United States.
It's difficult to determine the buyer's profile, as the notebook attracts both businessmen as well as students and professionals.
Like Moleskine, its users are cosmopolitan, open-spirited and cult.
Italy's small company, which had 12 workers in its infancy, now owns about 100 and, since 2008, it has two offices: one in Milan and the other in New York.
He distributes his notebooks in bookstores, from the Fnac to the small shops of the center of the cities, and has been able to impose his identity.
"We've adapted to the times and the need to write, despite the e-mail boom," explains Maria Sebregondi.
This cultural firm goes hand in hand with Moleskine's development: Recently, the brand has launched the exhibition "Detour" where architects or artists expose their own notebook.
In August, he also released a notebook with leopard tapas, to the Locarno Film Festival.
Taking advantage of its success and cultural aura, Moleskine has been able to diversify its products: handbags, agendas, pens, computer covers... The brand covers a wide range of supports.
An envoy-envious success: in the autumn of 2006, Mode Mode was acquired by the SG Capital Europe investment fund, currently Syntegra Capital, thus writing a new page in Moleskine's history.
Why is the unemployment rate for disabled workers still so high?
Around 19 per cent of disabled people are looking for a job.
The Fifteenth Week for the Employment of the Disabled begins today.
In spite of a resolved public policy, implemented since 2005, the unemployment rate among disabled persons doubles the average rate: 19 per cent, compared to 9 per cent for the entire population.
Disheartening?
"No," says Eric Blanchet, CEO of Adapt, the association that organizes the week for the employment of the disabled.
&quot; The progress made for fifteen years is considerable.
"At the time, the unemployment rate wasn't just three times higher than the average.
"However, there's still plenty to do."
It should be mentioned that the disabled continue to face numerous obstacles in access to employment.
They're at a later age: 37 per cent are more than 50 (about 17 per cent on average).
They have an unhigh level of qualification: 80 per per cent do not complete the baccalaureate and, of the 2.3 million students in higher education, only 10,000 are disabled.
Finally, long-lasting unemployment is widespread among them: 53 per cent for the disabled, compared with 38 per cent on average.
In other words, there are many issues that particularly hinder the return to the world of work and that "constitute the crux of the problem," says Christian Grapin, CEO of Tremplin, an association created in 1992 to help companies hire disabled workers.
That's the objective hurdles.
In addition, there are those that are not mentioned; the mentality of the people advances, but too slowly.
A study carried out in April by the IMS-Entreprendre association for Cité pointed out that the directors surveyed have "a level of knowledge about low disabilities," with an "excessive representation of people in the wheelchair, deficient physicists or intellectuals."
The result: private companies with more than 20 workers are a long way from the objective of hiring 6% of disabled workers, a target that was set out in a law... 1987.
According to the latest available figures, dating from 2008, they reach a ratio of 2.6 per cent.
"Now we reach around 3% ," says Pierre Blanc, CEO of Agefiph, the agency responsible for collecting contributions from companies that do not respect this law.
The test: the number of establishments "subject to this contribution," as well as the amounts disbursed, has decreased between 2007 and 2011, from 59,000 to 46,000 structures (from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The 2005 law, which increased contributions, has generated a lot of pressure for companies," Pierre Blanc explains.
"But this improvement also reflects real awareness, particularly among the large groups."
According to the Ministry of Labour, only 59 per cent of companies with between 20 and 49 workers have disabled employees, compared with 82 per cent of companies with between 100 and 199 workers.
Hazard: "A Joy"
On the verge of pitting against the Bleus on Tuesday night (20h45), Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens don't obscure the demeasuring pleasure this friendly match.
We won't dare to say connivance between them, but the image is symbolic.
Sitting beside each other, on the bench of the Stade de France auditorium, Georges Leekens and Eden Hazard have actually made amends.
Five months after his striker's grudge gesture against Turkey, the Belgian selector has nothing but praise for him.
"I was very happy for Eden, for his progress and because he's Belgian".
"I want to move forward," Lille's striker responds.
In terms of the contrast between his presentations in his team and in the selection, "it's a whole," it has been justified.
"In Lille, I'm all year round, which doesn't apply to Belgium.
"I've got to place myself".
Convened to play against Romania on Friday night, considering this match against France, Hazard comes to Saint-Denis to finally "shine."
Don't forget "Please".
He's been cheering for the Bleus since he was seven, as well as during the victorious campaigns of 1998 and 2000.
"I've always been his fan," she says.
Undoubtedly, that will especially reinforce the "particular" nature of the meeting.
Especially because he'll face Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami) ," he recalls.
"We've just faced each other recently.
"Playing against them is always a joy."
Simply, Leekens has made reference to "pride."
"Pride" for contesting "a gala game."
"Pringer" will also be verified, as "neighbor," that France "is again a team of very high level," about a year after Knysna's trauma.
"Laurent Blanc has done a formidable job," he said enthusiastically.
While Belgium willn't attend the up-to-or-to-European competition, the technicia technician would be happy to be able to share an airplane with the Bleus to Brazil in 2014.
"Our little Belgians are growing," he said.
