"You'll be able to see it in the next few matches," including the match against France.
Portugal shows plenty of confidence
Paulo Bento's Portugal is racing with great optimism to the back-to-back match against Bosnia (22h00), which will take place on Tuesday in Lisbon.
In the one-way match, both teams lacked realism.
"When these 90 minutes pass, we will be a happy team and, above all, a happy country."
Paulo Bento is already seen in Poland and in Ukraine.
There's no doubt about it; the Selecao, which he has led since September 2010, is going to qualify for the 2012 Euro Cup.
Indeed, only 90 minutes separate Portugal from happiness.
But there's a game to play and a rival, Bosnia, to defeat.
Considering the one-way match (0-0), on Friday in Zenica's hell, it seems clear that things are not decided.
Portugal dominated the game for more than an hour, yeah, but couldn't score the valuable goal that would have given him peace of mind.
"It's a good result, but at the same time it carries a risk, since we haven't scored away from home."
"But at home, in the luminous Stadium of the Light of Lisbon, the Portugues are convinced that they will pass, before an audience that should be equally enthusiastic, even if it is evident that the party "is very equal," dixit an optimist but lucid Beautiful.
"We're expecting the game to be very equal, but we're absolutely convinced we can take the lead," said the 42-year-old Portuguese coach.
"We continue to have a 50% chance to participate in the 2012 Euro Cup," said Carlos Queiroz.
Friday's tie in Bosnia forces the Portuguese to score and to win.
The bosses could get a tie, although they also need to score.
"It is a good result, but at the same time it carries a risk, since we haven't scored away from home," Bento laments.
"We are going to play against a team that has a great technical quality and that has players of great individual quality," he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese will go with the intention of exerting, from the beginning, a maximum pressure on the Bosnians to "leave them no time to think."
It's a nice idea, as long as it's realistic.
